shared date-keyed filename; fixed by giving each mode its own output path | 2026-09-02.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/handoff-doc-uncommitted-tooling-changes]] | Handoff with `memlint.py` and four supporting files left uncommitted by choice, a temp-path placement recurrence, and a cross-session \"another session may have fixed it\" check | 2026-09-02.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[connections/always-loaded-context-is-the-expensive-place-to-be-wrong]] | Wrongness, staleness, and volume are three costs on the same session-resident surface; consolidation concentrates the first unless the pointer's target is re-verified | 2026-09-02.md, 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/microsoft-forms-vs-google-forms-inventory-mismatch]] | Live inventory survey runs on Microsoft Forms while project docs point at 08-13-era Google Forms; a `gws` query there returns a truthful-but-false \"no new responses\", exposed only by the raw `.xlsx` header row | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/tier-a-memory-as-the-durable-payload-of-a-verification-session]] | A verification check's payload is a trap plus a command, so it routes to Tier A memory rather than a handoff doc; reinforced by the project not being a git repo | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[connections/a-stale-pointer-resolves-cleanly-to-the-wrong-answer]] | Stale Google Form IDs, a stale DEV memory router, and a nearly-dangling AGENTS.md pointer all succeed at resolution while returning the wrong answer; check from the target's side and stamp a verification date | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] | Adds the unpushed `dc54c97`/`38e462b` commits and the cross-link to `DexaProject` still living as a public GitHub repo with a patient CSV | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] | Adds the 78→86 job-count drift from 2026-09-15, restating the tell in its threshold-free form (single-digit okCount = SSD absent) | 2026-09-09.md, 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/error-log-archive-collision-overwrite]] | A `cp` to a date-keyed archive path destroyed three prior `.errors.log` lines (recovered from git); archive names must not collide or must append; `cp /dev/null` is the only working truncation route | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/session-transcript-tracking-repo-growth]] | Tracking session transcripts added ~368 MB to `~/.claude` and made repo size grow daily; left as an open decision because Tier B capture is a proven reconstruction fallback | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/the-preservation-step-must-itself-be-verified]] | Archive-before-clear and free-run-overwrites-paid both failed by encoding a once-daily assumption into a filename; the verified tarball discipline shows why an unchecked safety net is worse than none | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]] | `~/DEV` converted to thin repo `erash11/DEV` tracking 12 router files; `/*` block-all `.gitignore` as the load-bearing line, `CLAUDE.md` deliberately kept as a workspace map, Windows box confirmed active | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside]] | 21 public repos read inside: only `B_Ellis` and `cmj-norms-app` held real athlete data; 404-as-live bug and a Vercel-not-Pages miss in the liveness check; 18 flipped private | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] | 36 Windows/Mac conflicts collapse to 4 (26 CRLF-only, 6 Mac-newer generated); \"Mac wins\" would have discarded 324 unique forecast-log rows | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact]] | Repo names, conflict flags, and cleaned exports all over- and under-include at once; treat the cheap list as a work queue, classify by kind before resolving | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] | Revisions supersede, accumulating records diverge; the correct verb is merge or append, and a smaller newer copy is the tell | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]] | ~200 `.from-*` conflicts traced to a Windows save command mangling encoding and prepending BOMs; proved on `portfolio.json` (9 updates matched, nothing lost), root cause fixed, 95 BOM files left alone | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/backup-oom-kill-during-first-time-index]] | Backup OOM-killed during a 20-min first-time index of `dev-docs` (547 files); index stale not corrupt, two dead-PID locks cleared, CPU% rejected as a health signal | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/backup-manifest-symlink-false-alarm]] | `manifest-unmanaged:_DRIVE` was a symlink, not a missing repo; backup taught to skip symlinks, two real gaps added with a 0 lost / 0 changed / 2 added proof | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] | Four \"not a repo\" folders were parent-tracked, five \"missing\" repos were name mismatches, two `repo-diverged` errors had self-cleared; verify before `gh repo create` | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/git-submodule-branch-pinning-decision]] | `ZoteroInjestion` made a real submodule but pinned to `mac-snapshot-2026-09-09` rather than `main`; a snapshot pin decays silently, decision deferred | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] | Mangled sync saves, a symlink-blind manifest check, and a backup committing its own output: recurrence is the tell that the fix belongs in the generator | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[connections/a-killed-process-leaves-debris-that-blocks-the-retry]] | Dead-PID locks and orphaned indexers are not self-healing; a loud crash becomes an indefinitely quiet no-op unless the kill's leavings are enumerated | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/harness-agnostic-aios-plan]] | Multi-task Mac+Windows skills/backup plan: 42 own vs 19 third-party skills reframing, Task 4 inversion script deleted, Task 2 prune 306→208 as a reversible move, three open round-10 findings | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/gitignore-trailing-slash-misses-symlinks]] | A `.gitignore` pattern ending in `/` matches directories but not symlinks, so the rule silently no-ops and an automated backup re-adds the entries; plus `.gitignore` vs `.git/info/exclude` scope | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/backup-verification-by-restore]] | Restore test returned 57 of 61 skills, exposing a two-hop symlink chain into an unbacked-up `~/.agents/vendor-review/`; only an actual restore can measure coverage the backup's own records cannot | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/cross-provider-review-gate]] | A rate-limit-forced switch from Codex to Claude found the plan's biggest error; resume cannot cross providers, and a prior approval is stale after an amendment (5 new bugs in the amendment itself) | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/submodule-conversion-breaks-rsync-backup]] | Submodule conversion turned `.git` from directory to file; rsync cannot overwrite a destination directory with a file, failing only while the SSD is mounted; fixed by rename, `okCount: 80` | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/a-type-change-at-the-source-breaks-the-mirror]] | Adds the Codex-skills symlink refusal as a second rsync type-change instance, making recurrence the argument for fixing the tooling | 2026-09-12.md, 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[connections/change-the-checker-down-or-sideways]] | Replacing a failed check with a primitive substrate works only where a ground truth exists; otherwise the only lever is a peer with uncorrelated blind spots. | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/the-execution-context-silently-edits-the-work-set]] | Session lifetime and mount state both silently shrink what a run attempts without producing an error, and both are fixed by moving the verdict onto a durable artifact that publishes cardinality. | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/test-the-layer-before-blaming-the-artifact]] | A failed upload or render usually indicts the transport layer, not the file — hold the artifact fixed and remove a stage before rebuilding anything | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory]] | Windows laptop had no working backup: ~15 GB single-copy, nine Disabled tasks, two triggerless ones, and the Mac's manifest installed as Windows'; `/MIR`→`/E` fix | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/windows-backup-task-trigger-gotchas]] | Four Task Scheduler gotchas: `schtasks /Change` can't add a schedule, triggerless tasks show `Ready` forever, `G:` is per-user so SYSTEM writes nothing, sleeping laptops miss fixed-time triggers | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/fix-from-the-working-machine-handoff]] | Adds the 2026-09-21 instance where *verification* was handed over as one command with a pre-stated expected output | 2026-09-14.md, 2026-09-21.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/verify-the-diagnosis-in-a-received-report]] | A received recovery report's findings held but one explanation didn't: `dev-sync` skips an enumerated list, not underscore-prefixed folders, so `_INBOX` was already a clean pushed repo | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/unattended-cross-machine-push-collisions]] | Three Windows pushes mid-session each needing a merge, including a deliberate-looking 913-file skill audit cutting ~160→63; accepted because git retains deletions, intent still to be confirmed | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[connections/every-layer-reported-healthy-except-the-destination]] | `Ready`, `status: OK`, and a backup's own records were each locally truthful while coverage failed; narrow stage predicates don't compose into end-to-end assurance | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/rsync-symlink-conversion-stale-directory]] | Codex skills converted to symlinks while the SSD held real directories; rsync cannot replace a directory with a symlink, fixed by moving four folders to `_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/` | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/manifest-classification-not-inferable-from-names]] | Six Baylor-sounding repos (`VALD_Performance`, `RTP_ProgressReport`, …) were personal, not work; manifest classification must be asked, not inferred | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]] | `.errors.log` archived and cleared only after a verified 0-error/86-job re-run — fixes are not their own confirmation | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/vendored-copy-drift-within-a-repo]] | `LibraryProject`'s embedded `ZoteroInjestion` one commit behind the standalone repo; `STATUS.md` missing its Next section with an unmerged Windows copy pasted at the bottom | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/mac-owned-data-pulls-sleep-lateness]] | Adds the 2026-09-21 cutover session: Windows-side three-line verification, the no-op 8 PM Windows task, and the pmset wake recommended then declined | 2026-09-21.md, 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/duckdb-snapshot-merge-by-primary-key]] | `load_snapshot.py` silently deleted 12,750 Mac-only 2024–2025 rows because a 2026-only check passed; rewritten test-first to merge by primary key, restored from its own pre-load backup | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/claude-code-stack-config-audit-2026-09-22]] | 50-session + 303-session audit: 5 zero-use plugins proposed for disabling, `: \"` in YAML breaking a whole SKILL.md header, dcg hook dead on Windows (913 failures, Mac path), skill-router 68 s worst case | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]] | 146-repo sync: union merge for append-only transcript logs, backup manifest not auto-discovering `TOS_Evidence_Brief`, Tier A memory not crossing machines, `compile.py` (~$1.28) left as the user's call | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[connections/verify-across-every-partition-the-write-touched]] | A 2026-only row check, an exit-code-only pull check, and the three-check inventory counter-example: verification scope must span every dimension the operation could change | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[connections/a-guard-that-cannot-run-fails-open]] | The dead Windows dcg hook and the never-fired Perch/VALD alerts both look identical to success; protective mechanisms need a liveness signal and a deliberate periodic trip | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/decommissioned-leg-verified-by-skipped-markers]] | Retired steps left in `refresh_all_data.bat` as greppable `skipped` markers make a cutover checkable by expected line count (3); the still-registered 8 PM no-op task is the residual reporting hazard | 2026-09-21.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/deferred-credential-rotation-recorded-rationale]] | Exposed VALD client secret deliberately not rotated (private repo, pulls work) and recorded as \"skip for now\"; a defensible deferral missing the falsifiable revisit trigger its sibling had | 2026-09-21.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[connections/write-the-decision-where-each-consumer-will-look]] | One handoff plus four per-repo `STATUS.md` records: placement follows how the next reader arrives at the question, and deferrals need the record more than completed work does | 2026-09-21.md | 2026-09-24 |\n\n\n## Related Wiki Articles\n\n### concepts/git-sync-status-versus-uncommitted-work.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"`synced (ahead=0)` Does Not Mean Today's Edits Are Committed\"\naliases: [ahead=0 misreading, synced but uncommitted, launchctl kickstart dailybackup, stale exit code clears only on scheduled run, wait for 09:00 or kick it, nightly backup auto-commits]\ntags: [git, backup, launchd, automation, verification, gotcha, macos]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-07.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-07\nupdated: 2026-09-07\n---\n\n# `synced (ahead=0)` Does Not Mean Today's Edits Are Committed\n\nA 2026-09-07 config-hygiene session left every edit uncommitted on the expectation that the 09:00 nightly backup would commit and push the skills repo automatically. The session recorded two adjacent status-reading traps: `synced (ahead=0)` reports that nothing is *queued to push*, which is trivially true when nothing has been committed at all; and `com.keystone.dailybackup`'s exit code 1 is stale and clears only on a launchd-initiated run, so manual `/backup` invocations leave it red regardless of outcome.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **`ahead=0` counts committed-but-unpushed commits.** A working tree full of uncommitted edits produces the same `synced (ahead=0)` as a genuinely clean, fully-backed-up repository — the indicator is blind to the working tree.\n- **Deferring commits to the nightly job is a deliberate, defensible choice** (it avoids duplicating the automation's work), but it means the window between the edit and 09:00 has the edits on exactly one disk while the sync indicator reads green. Both of this evening's sessions — the config edits and the `MEMORY.md` trim — were left in that window simultaneously.\n- **A stale launchd exit code only clears on a launchd-initiated run.** Manual `/backup` invocations do not reset `com.keystone.dailybackup`'s column-2 status, which stays 1 until the next scheduled tick or an explicit `launchctl kickstart`.\n- **The forcing option was named explicitly:** `launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.keystone.dailybackup` (~7 minutes) versus simply waiting for 09:00. The question was left open with the user rather than decided unilaterally.\n- **Unexplained extra runs are worth chasing to a cause.** Additional 07:00 and 07:11 backup runs were resolved as manual user invocations rather than a scheduling defect — a benign explanation, but only after checking.\n- **A rollback path was recorded alongside the change:** `git -C ~/.claude checkout CLAUDE.md AGENTS.md .gitignore && rm ~/.claude/TOOLPATHS.md`, with the prior DEV file backed up under the session's job tmp directory.\n\n## Details\n\nThe `ahead=0` misreading belongs to the family of status fields whose predicate is narrower than the question being asked of them, described in [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]]. The field faithfully answers \"are there local commits the remote lacks?\" and is read as \"is my work backed up?\" The two coincide only when the working tree is clean, and the deferred-commit workflow adopted here guarantees they diverge for most of the day. The practical consequence is that a durability check must inspect the working tree — `git status`, not the ahead/behind counter — and that the answer to \"is today's work safe?\" during the deferral window is genuinely no, at least until the CRASHBOX SSD leg captures uncommitted files as documented in [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]]. That leg exists precisely for this class of content, which is why its completeness matters more on days like this one, when a full evening of `~/.claude` and `~/DEV` edits sat uncommitted by design.\n\nThe launchd exit-code behavior restates and sharpens the finding from [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]]: column 2 tracks scheduled invocations only. The new detail is the escape hatch — `launchctl kickstart -k` initiates a run *through launchd*, so unlike a manual `/backup` it does update the status field, at the cost of about seven minutes. That gives a genuine choice rather than a resignation: wait for the schedule and let the code self-clear, or force a launchd-initiated run that both commits the day's edits and resets the indicator in one pass. The session correctly framed this as a decision for the user, since the tradeoff is between latency and a few minutes of machine time, not between correct and incorrect.\n\nThe extra-runs investigation is a small but instructive counterpoint to the session's other findings. Two unexplained backup executions at 07:00 and 07:11 could plausibly have been a duplicate trigger, a retry loop, or a misconfigured plist — all defects worth fixing. The actual cause was the user running them by hand. The cheapest resolution to an anomaly in a scheduled system is often to ask whether a human caused it, and doing so before instrumenting the scheduler avoids the failure mode where a benign event drives a real change to working automation. Recording the rollback command in the same breath as the change is the complementary habit: it is cheapest to write down while the change set is still in working memory, and it is the only thing that makes an uncommitted, unbacked-up edit set safely reversible.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — The original scheduled-run-only exit-code finding; this session adds the `launchctl kickstart` escape hatch that does update it.\n- [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]] — The SSD leg that covers uncommitted files, and therefore the only protection during a deferred-commit window.\n- [[concepts/canonical-section-relocation-inbound-pointers]] — The edits that were deliberately left uncommitted in this session.\n- [[concepts/memory-cap-relief-by-migration-not-deletion]] — The same evening's `MEMORY.md` trim, likewise left for the 09:00 job.\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — The health check that reported the repos synced, and the routine within which this status field is normally read.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — The general shape: `ahead=0` and the launchd exit code both answer narrower predicates than \"is my work safe?\"\n- [[connections/background-task-exit-code-false-failure]] — The neighbouring discipline of reconciling exit codes against durable artifacts rather than trusting them.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-07.md]] — Config-hygiene session (18:05): all edits left uncommitted for the 09:00 nightly backup to auto-commit and push the skills repo, `synced (ahead=0)` clarified as meaning nothing is queued to push rather than that the day's edits are committed, `com.keystone.dailybackup` exit code 1 confirmed stale and clearable only by a launchd-initiated run, the `launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.keystone.dailybackup` (~7 min) option left open with the user, extra 07:00/07:11 runs resolved as manual user invocations, and the recorded rollback path. Stack-doctor session (18:13): the `MEMORY.md` trim likewise left uncommitted for the same 09:00 job.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"DEV Sync 2026-09-22: Union-Merge Transcripts, Manifest Non-Discovery, and Cross-Machine Memory\"\naliases: [union merge transcripts, TOS_Evidence_Brief missing from manifest, 146 repos walked, Linda Projects space breaks sync step, Tier A does not cross machines, backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22, 22 active 117 dormant]\ntags: [git, sync, backup, macos, memory, workflow, verification]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-22.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-22\nupdated: 2026-09-24\n---\n\n# DEV Sync 2026-09-22: Union-Merge Transcripts, Manifest Non-Discovery, and Cross-Machine Memory\n\nA `/dev-sync` run on 2026-09-22 walked 146 repositories to bring the Mac in line with GitHub: two pulled (`_INBOX`, `DataBase_GPS_Reporting`), one pushed (`CommandCentre`), one cloned (`TOS_Evidence_Brief`, created on Windows 9/21), with 22 active and 117 dormant-and-clean, and no conflicts. Its durable findings concern how recurring cross-machine friction is resolved: a merge strategy for append-only logs, a manifest that does not discover its own new inputs, and the fact that Tier A memory does not travel between machines.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Union merge is the right strategy for append-only transcript logs.** Mac/Windows conflicts in `global-memory/transcripts/*.md` resolve cleanly by keeping every line from both machines, producing no `.from-*` files to clean up afterward.\n- **The backup manifest does not auto-discover new repos.** `TOS_Evidence_Brief` — created on Windows the previous day — was absent from `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`, which made the 09:05 backup report an error. Adding it fixed the report (backup of the prior file at `backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22`).\n- **Tier A memory does not cross machines.** Durable GPS decisions were therefore written into the repo's own `docs/STATUS.md` *and* `global-memory/MEMORY.md` so the Windows machine would see them.\n- **A folder name containing a space (`DEV/Linda Projects`) broke a sync step again** and had to be redone with the path quoted — a recurrence of the same hazard seen on 2026-09-11.\n- **`compile.py` was deliberately not run.** A compile costs ~$1.28, so clearing the 2-log memory-compiler backlog was left as the user's call rather than taken as an auto-action; the warning persists in `.errors.log` until then.\n\n## Details\n\nThe union-merge finding names a resolution strategy that fits the artifact's semantics rather than a generic \"take one side\" rule. A transcript log is append-only and each machine appends independently, so the two versions are not competing revisions of the same content — they are disjoint additions to a shared record. Under that structure the correct merge keeps every line, and the usual conflict-file machinery (`.from-*` copies awaiting manual adjudication) is pure overhead because there is nothing to adjudicate. This is the same classify-by-kind-before-resolving discipline applied in [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]], where treating accumulating records as revisions would have discarded 324 unique forecast-log rows, and the same accumulate-versus-supersede distinction generalized in [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]]. Notably, the same day's PFF loader bug was the identical error one layer down — a replace where a merge was required.\n\nThe manifest gap is a recurrence rather than a novelty, and its recurrence is the point. A repo created on one machine does not announce itself to a backup manifest maintained on another; the manifest's coverage is declared, not discovered, so a new repo is invisible to it until a human adds a line. The 09:05 backup error was in this case an honest signal — the manifest check did fire — but the general hazard is the one catalogued in [[connections/silent-omission-defeats-per-stage-success]] and [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]]: every managed item succeeds while the unmanaged item is never counted. Prior instances at [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] and [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] make this at least the third occurrence, which by the reasoning in [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] is the threshold at which the fix belongs in the discovery mechanism rather than in another manual manifest edit.\n\nThe Tier A observation is a routing constraint with immediate practical force. Tier A memory is local to the machine that wrote it, so any decision that a second machine must honor — here, that the Mac owns the pulls and the Windows GPS writer is stopped — cannot live there alone. Writing it into the repository's `docs/STATUS.md` puts it in a location git already replicates, and mirroring it into `global-memory/MEMORY.md` reaches the synced memory surface; between them the decision arrives on the other machine by two independent paths. This extends the content-to-tier routing matrix in [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] with a cross-machine axis, and matches the working-machine-authors-the-fix pattern in [[concepts/fix-from-the-working-machine-handoff]]. Finally, the deliberate refusal to run `compile.py` is a recorded non-action rather than an oversight: a ~$1.28 spend is a user decision, so the backlog warning was left standing in `.errors.log` with the command documented in the compiler's README — consistent with treating `.errors.log` as an escalation channel that clears only on a verified clean run.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] — The prior conflict triage that established classifying by kind before resolving; union merge is the resolution for the accumulating-record kind.\n- [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] — Why an append-only log must be merged rather than overwritten.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — An earlier instance of the same declared-not-discovered manifest gap.\n- [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] — The fail-closed manifest guard designed against exactly this omission.\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — The general shape: an unlisted item cannot fail a check it was never part of.\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] — The 2026-09-11 sync where `Linda Projects`' embedded space first broke the parallel fetch loop.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — The tier-routing matrix this session extends with a cross-machine constraint.\n- [[concepts/compiler-backlog-hook-notice]] — The backlog warning deliberately left in `.errors.log`.\n- [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]] — Why the warning stays until a verified clean compile, not until the fix is merely intended.\n- [[concepts/mac-owned-data-pulls-sleep-lateness]] — The GPS decisions whose cross-machine visibility motivated the dual write.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-22.md]] — Sync and closeout session (17:25): `/dev-sync` walking 146 repos with `_INBOX` and `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` pulled, `CommandCentre` pushed, `TOS_Evidence_Brief` cloned, 22 active / 117 dormant / no conflicts; union merge resolving Mac/Windows transcript-log conflicts without `.from-*` files; `TOS_Evidence_Brief` missing from `backup-manifest.json` causing the 09:05 backup error, fixed with a `.bak-2026-09-22` backup; `DEV/Linda Projects` space breaking a sync step; Tier A memory not crossing machines, resolved by writing to `docs/STATUS.md` plus `global-memory/MEMORY.md`; `compile.py` (~$1.28) deliberately left as the user's call.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Closing a 49-Folder Backup Manifest Coverage Gap\"\naliases: [49 unmanifested DEV folders, backup-manifest.json 78 to 125, job count 28 to 76, manifest coverage gap, AI_Analyst token-dashboard removed, DexaProject Google Drive warning, migration left folders unmanifested]\ntags: [backup, automation, macos, keystoneos, verification, maintenance, git]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-09.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-09\nupdated: 2026-09-09\n---\n\n# Closing a 49-Folder Backup Manifest Coverage Gap\n\nOn 2026-09-09 a KeystoneOS daily backup run returned `status: ERROR` with two problems: the `~/.claude` repo was one commit behind GitHub (blocking its push) and 49 folders under `~/DEV` were absent from `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`. Rather than triage the 49, the user confirmed that all of them mattered — the gap was a residue of an earlier migration — and every one was added. The manifest grew from 78 to 125 entries and the backup's job count went from 28 to 76.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The gap was invisible.** Folders absent from the manifest are never backed up, and their absence produces no error that reads as urgent — only an advisory `manifest-unmanaged` class. The 49-folder hole had existed since a prior migration.\n- **All 49 were added, not triaged:** 47 as git repos (working-tree capture to the CRASHBOX SSD) and 2 as plain folders (`_ARCHIVE`, `docs` — SSD copy only, no repo semantics).\n- **Two stale entries were removed** (`AI_Analyst`, `token-dashboard`) after confirming both had already moved into `~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/`, leaving 125 entries. Manifest hygiene runs in both directions: adding new folders and retiring moved ones.\n- **`DexaProject`'s warning was kept deliberately** — it lives on Google Drive intentionally, so its manifest complaint is an accepted permanent condition rather than a defect to silence. A separate later finding in the same day established that the *GitHub* copy of `DexaProject` still holds a patient CSV and must be deleted.\n- **The `~/.claude` behind-by-one state was resolved with `git pull --rebase --autostash`,** preserving two uncommitted edits (`settings.json`, `hooks/herdr-agent-state.sh`). Backups never auto-merge by design.\n- **Commits were made but not pushed** — `~/.claude` at `dc54c97` and `~/.claude/global-memory` at `38e462b` — with the push deliberately left to the next scheduled backup run.\n\n## Details\n\nThis is the largest instance yet of the manifest-drift failure class first catalogued in [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] (three folders) and re-observed at [[concepts/backup-error-log-recurring-failures-2026-08-16]] (five folders). The escalation from three to five to forty-nine is informative: the earlier gaps were ordinary drift from folders created faster than manifest entries, whereas this one had a single structural cause — a migration that moved or created many directories at once while the manifest, being hand-maintained, stayed where it was. Bulk moves are precisely the event a curated list cannot survive, which strengthens the standing proposal in [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] to derive the manifest from a directory scan rather than maintaining it by hand. The default-deny guard did its job in that the folders were reported rather than silently excluded, but reporting alone is only sufficient when the report is acted on, and forty-nine entries accumulating across weeks shows the report being observed and deferred.\n\nThe removal of `AI_Analyst` and `token-dashboard` is the neglected half of manifest maintenance. A manifest entry pointing at a folder that has moved into `_ARCHIVE` will either fail loudly (noise) or resolve to a stale path (worse), and neither outcome is caught by any check that only asks \"is every folder covered?\" The `DexaProject` decision demonstrates the third state a manifest entry can occupy: an accepted, documented exception whose warning is expected output rather than a pending task. Recording the reason — it lives on Google Drive intentionally — is what prevents a future maintenance pass from either \"fixing\" it or being confused by it, the same discipline applied to the two large graph files kept deliberately in the 2026-09-01 classification pass. Notably, the same day's repo-visibility audit found that this deliberate local exception coexists with a genuine problem at a different layer: `erash11/DexaProject` is still a GitHub repository containing a patient CSV, awaiting deletion. A documented exception on one axis does not clear the artifact on every axis.\n\nThe `git pull --rebase --autostash` choice is worth isolating as policy. The backup job pushes several repos, and a repo that is behind its remote cannot push; the tempting resolutions are a force-push (destroys remote history) or a merge (creates a commit the operator did not author). Rebasing with autostash preserves both the remote's history and the two uncommitted working-tree edits, and the stated principle — backups never auto-merge — keeps the backup system from making content decisions on the operator's behalf. This is the same restraint shown when a two-writer SecondBrain conflict was resolved by staging both sides rather than adjudicating it, per [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]]. The decision to commit `~/.claude` and `global-memory` without pushing follows the same logic in the other direction: the push is the backup's job, so leaving it to the next scheduled run keeps one owner for that action rather than racing it.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] — The fail-closed guard that surfaced the 49 folders; this run is the strongest argument yet for its auto-derivation follow-up.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — The original three-folder instance of the same failure class.\n- [[concepts/backup-error-log-recurring-failures-2026-08-16]] — The five-folder `manifest-unmanaged` instance between that one and this.\n- [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] — The completeness metric this session reset from 28 to 76.\n- [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]] — The job-count completeness tell, which this session extends and complicates by moving the baseline.\n- [[concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline]] — The SSD leg that now receives working-tree captures for 47 additional repos.\n- [[concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside]] — The same day's finding that `DexaProject` is also a live public GitHub repo holding a patient CSV.\n- [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]] — The precedent for resolving repo divergence without adjudicating content on the operator's behalf.\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — The general shape: a clean run over a work set that was never complete.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-09.md]] — Backup repair session (19:17): initial run `status: ERROR` with `~/.claude` one commit behind and 49 unmanifested `~/DEV` folders; user confirmed all 49 mattered (\"we did a migration earlier\"); 47 added as git repos and 2 as plain folders (`_ARCHIVE`, `docs`); job count 28 → 76 and manifest 78 → 125 entries; `AI_Analyst` and `token-dashboard` removed after confirming both had moved to `~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/`; `DexaProject` warning kept as an intentional Google Drive exception; `~/.claude` fixed with `git pull --rebase --autostash` preserving two uncommitted edits; commits `dc54c97` and `38e462b` left unpushed for the next backup run.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Windows Laptop Backup Failure and RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md\"\naliases: [RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md, Windows no working backup, ~15 GB single copy, nine disabled KeystoneOS tasks, Mac manifest on Windows, FIX-WINDOWS-BACKUP.md, backup_dev.ps1 MIR to E]\ntags: [backup, windows, macos, automation, keystoneos, scheduling, data-integrity, migration]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-14.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-14\nupdated: 2026-09-14\n---\n\n# Windows Laptop Backup Failure and RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md\n\nOn 2026-09-14 a `RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md` commit arriving from the Windows laptop revealed that the machine had **no working backup at all**. Roughly 15 GB exists in a single copy only — `SportScienceResearchRepo` (4.37 GB), `Football` (2.94 GB), `Risk_Stratification_Engine` (1.80 GB), `ForcePlate_DecisionSystem` (1.69 GB), and the `~/.codex` history. Everything else — all 131 repos, `~/.claude`, skills, global memory, and Google Drive documents — is safe. The failure had three independent root causes, all of them artifacts of the Mac migration.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Nine KeystoneOS Windows scheduled tasks are Disabled** — switched off during the move to the Mac and never restored. They report their disabled state accurately; nothing ever escalated it.\n- **Two replacement tasks were registered with no trigger.** A triggerless task shows `Ready` indefinitely and will never fire — the healthiest-looking possible state for a job that does nothing.\n- **The Windows backup manifest is the Mac's manifest.** It carries `/Volumes/CRASHBOX` and `/Users/kristinarash/...` paths, with its last state written 2026-08-14 at status ERROR. `~/.claude/scripts/daily-backup.ps1` had likewise been overwritten by the Mac version (rsync + Mac paths) on 2026-08-05; the original is frozen at `daily-backup.ps1.win-orig`.\n- **`backup_dev.ps1` was changed from `/MIR` to `/E`.** Mirroring propagates accidental deletions into the backup, which converts the backup into a second copy of a mistake rather than a recovery path. (`backup_ai_sessions.ps1` was already `/E`.)\n- **Two deliberate non-actions:** the backup scripts stay in `DEV\\_INBOX\\` because the registered scheduled task points there, and the nine disabled tasks are *not* re-enabled because the script they invoke is now the Mac's.\n\n## Details\n\nWhat makes this a distinct failure rather than another manifest-drift instance is that every layer reported truthfully and none of them reported the operational fact. A disabled task is disabled; a triggerless task is genuinely ready; a manifest containing Mac paths is a syntactically valid manifest. The only signal that would have surfaced the problem is the one nobody was checking: whether bytes actually landed in Google Drive. This is the same scope mismatch catalogued in [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]], where `launchctl list` reports only the last *scheduled* run — the field answers a narrower predicate than the operator's question and is therefore silent about the gap. The recovery report's own conclusion states the rule directly: verification must confirm bytes at the destination, not that the task looks healthy.\n\nThe three causes also share a single origin. Tasks disabled \"during the Mac move,\" a manifest overwritten with Mac paths on 08-14, a `daily-backup.ps1` overwritten on 08-05 — the migration edited the Windows machine's automation surface as a side effect of syncing shared files, and nothing on the Windows side was left running to notice. This extends the pattern in [[connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine]] in the opposite direction: that article covers Windows assumptions surviving onto the Mac, whereas here Mac-shaped configuration flowed back onto Windows through the same shared repositories. Bidirectional sync between two machines with platform-specific automation means any file that encodes host facts — paths, mount points, shell dialects — is a collision waiting to happen, which is why the new `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.windows.json` was created as a *separate* file rather than a re-edit of the shared one.\n\nThe `/MIR` → `/E` change deserves isolation as policy. Robocopy's `/MIR` makes the destination an exact mirror of the source, which means a local deletion is faithfully reproduced in the backup at the next run — the backup is then a copy of the current state rather than a record of history. `/E` copies without deleting, accepting stale files in exchange for never propagating a loss. The trade-off resolves the same way as the append-only forecast log in [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]]: when the artifact's value is that it holds something the source might no longer have, the correct verb is never \"make identical.\"\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/windows-backup-task-trigger-gotchas]] — The four scheduling mechanics that make a Windows backup task silently ineffective, and the paste-ready fix page.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — `Ready`, `Disabled`, and a valid-but-wrong manifest all report accurately on a narrower predicate than \"is the data backed up?\"\n- [[connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine]] — The mirror-image case: platform-specific configuration surviving a migration, here flowing Mac → Windows.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — The Mac-side manifest whose paths ended up on the Windows machine.\n- [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] — The companion lesson that a backup's own status field cannot establish coverage.\n- [[connections/the-execution-context-silently-edits-the-work-set]] — A triggerless task and a SYSTEM-context missing `G:` both shrink the work set without producing an error.\n- [[concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline]] — The Mac-side SSD leg; its `/backup` run was still outstanding at session close.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-14.md]] — Memory maintenance session (22:05): `RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md` arriving from the Windows laptop; ~15 GB in single copy only (SportScienceResearchRepo 4.37 GB, Football 2.94 GB, Risk_Stratification_Engine 1.80 GB, ForcePlate_DecisionSystem 1.69 GB, `~/.codex` history) with 131 repos, `~/.claude`, skills, global memory, and Drive docs safe; three root causes (nine Disabled KeystoneOS tasks, two triggerless replacement tasks showing `Ready`, Windows manifest actually the Mac's with `/Volumes/CRASHBOX` and `/Users/kristinarash/...` paths at status ERROR 2026-08-14); `backup_dev.ps1` changed `/MIR` → `/E`; `backup-manifest.windows.json` created as documentation only; scripts kept in `DEV\\_INBOX\\` and the nine tasks left disabled because `daily-backup.ps1` was overwritten by the Mac version on 2026-08-05 (original at `daily-backup.ps1.win-orig`).\n\n```\n\n### connections/memory-compiler-tier-independence-under-failure.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Tier B Transcript Capture Survives a Day-Long Tier C Compiler Failure\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture\"\n  - \"concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-13.md\"\ncreated: 2026-06-13\nupdated: 2026-06-16\n---\n\n# Connection: Tier B Transcript Capture Survives a Day-Long Tier C Compiler Failure\n\n## The Connection\n\nThe three-tier Claude Code memory architecture ([[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]]) claims that its tiers operate independently — a failure in one does not cascade to the others. On 2026-06-13, this claim was empirically tested under real conditions. The Tier C compiler ([[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]]) produced FLUSH_ERRORs on every invocation throughout the day due to `claude-agent-sdk` version skew (bundled CLI 2.1.92 vs system 2.1.177). Despite 100% Tier C failure across three separate flush attempts, Tier B — the `transcript-capture.js` Stop-hook scratchpad — continued operating without interruption. By the end of the day, 24 days of continuous Tier B logs were intact and four sessions from the failure day itself had been captured.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nThe architectural independence of the three tiers is not merely a design claim — it was demonstrated empirically under a full-day, complete-failure scenario in Tier C. The key structural reason Tier B survived is that it has a minimal, self-contained dependency stack: `transcript-capture.js` is a JavaScript file that writes to a local directory on each Stop event. It has no dependency on `claude-agent-sdk`, no API version constraints, and no external process invocation that could be broken by a Claude Code update. Tier C's compile layer, by contrast, is the most complex tier: it invokes the `claude-agent-sdk` bundled CLI to run LLM synthesis, and that CLI must match the system Claude Code version to parse the current `settings.json` correctly.\n\nThe non-obvious operational implication is that raw transcript context is preserved in Tier B even when Tier C's curated synthesis layer is completely dark. A version-skew failure that produces 100% FLUSH_ERRORs does not mean the day's sessions are lost — it means the Decisions/Lessons/Action-Items extraction did not happen. The raw text is intact in Tier B transcripts and can be backfilled after the version-skew fix is applied (via `uv lock --upgrade-package claude-agent-sdk && uv sync`). The 2026-06-13 Ath_Medicine session (15:34–16:46) was reconstructed exactly this way: the raw Tier B transcript (`global-memory/transcripts/2026-06-13.md`) provided the source material for a manual backfill appended to the daily log. This recovery path — Tier B as a durable fallback for Tier C reconstruction — is now a known and tested operational procedure, not just a theoretical possibility.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2026-06-13 (13:18, 16:10, 16:37): Three consecutive FLUSH_ERRORS — 100% failure rate on Tier C compiler invocations throughout the day. Root cause: `claude-agent-sdk==0.1.56` bundling CLI 2.1.92, system at 2.1.177.\n- 2026-06-13 (17:26 Memory Flush): \"Tier B raw transcript capture (`transcript-capture.js` Stop hook) is healthy and independent of the compiler — 24 days of continuous logs; today already had 4 sessions captured.\"\n- 2026-06-13 NOTE: \"The substantive work lost in that window — the Ath_Medicine session [8aa53443], 15:34–16:46 — is reconstructed below from the raw Tier B transcript (`global-memory/transcripts/2026-06-13.md`).\"\n- The 13:18 flush failure covered only a minor KeystoneOS memory-save confirmation (drive-topology), already persisted as Tier A `project-drive-topology.md` — demonstrating that Tier A is also independent of both B and C.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — The three-tier architecture whose independence claim was empirically validated; the \"failure in one does not cascade to the others\" property is now backed by a concrete full-day failure scenario.\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — The Tier C compile layer that failed, including the version-skew root cause and fix (`uv lock --upgrade-package claude-agent-sdk && uv sync`), and the diagnostic caution about not invoking the bundled claude.exe to check version state.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Cross-Machine Conflict File Triage: 36 Files Collapse to 4\"\naliases: [Windows Mac conflict files, 26 CRLF-only conflicts, graphify-out portfolio.json Mac newer, weekly_forecast_log.csv 324 rows, mac wins dangerous for append-only logs, newer is not more complete, SoftwareTech DRAFT 1.0 MB vs 0.4 MB]\ntags: [git, sync, windows, macos, data-integrity, verification, gotcha]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-09.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-09\nupdated: 2026-09-09\n---\n\n# Cross-Machine Conflict File Triage: 36 Files Collapse to 4\n\nThirty-six files flagged as conflicting between a Windows machine and a Mac were diffed individually on 2026-09-09 and reduced to four genuine conflicts. Twenty-six differed only by CRLF line endings and six were `graphify-out/` or `portfolio.json` artifacts where the Mac copy is simply newer. The residue produced the session's most consequential finding: a blanket \"Mac wins\" resolution rule silently discards data when the file is an append-only log.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **26 of 36 conflicts were CRLF-only** — a Windows line-ending artifact rather than a content difference, and safe to delete without inspection once identified.\n- **6 more were `graphify-out/` and `portfolio.json` generated files** where the Mac copy is newer and the Windows copy has no independent value.\n- **The 32 safe files can be deleted with zero risk;** only four require human comparison.\n- **\"Mac wins\" is wrong for append-only logs.** `CFB_Victory_Model/data/weekly_forecast_log.csv` is *smaller* on the Mac; the Windows copy holds 324 forecast rows dating back to 2026-08-26 that exist nowhere else. Newer does not mean more complete.\n- **Three `SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework/deliverables/` Word/Excalidraw files remain open**, with the DRAFT at 1.0 MB on Windows against 0.4 MB on the Mac — a size gap large enough that neither timestamp nor size alone should decide it.\n\n## Details\n\nThe 89% collapse rate is itself the first lesson: a conflict *count* produced by a sync tool measures byte-level divergence, which is a far weaker property than semantic divergence. CRLF normalization has repeatedly made this stack's working trees lie toward difference, and the same mechanism inflates any cross-machine conflict list generated on a repository that has passed through Windows. Acting on the raw count of 36 would have meant 36 manual comparisons; diffing first cut the human work by an order of magnitude and, more importantly, concentrated attention on the four files where judgment was actually required. The generalizable move is to classify the conflict list by *kind of difference* before resolving any individual entry.\n\nThe forecast-log finding is the durable one and it inverts an intuition that most sync tooling encodes as a default. Last-writer-wins is a reasonable rule for a document, where the newer version is a revision of the older and contains it. It is actively destructive for an append-only log, where the two copies are not revisions of one another but divergent branches of an accumulating record — each machine appended its own rows, and the correct resolution is a *merge*, not a selection. That the Mac copy was smaller is the tell that should trigger the check, and it is a tell that a timestamp-based rule cannot see by construction. The 324 Windows-only rows back to 2026-08-26 are exactly the kind of data whose loss would be discovered months later, during an evaluation, as an unexplained gap — the same class of silent, delayed damage as the `.errors.log` archive overwrite from earlier the same day, and recoverable there only because git happened to hold a second copy.\n\nThe three unresolved `SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework` deliverables illustrate the boundary of automated triage. Word and Excalidraw files are opaque binaries whose size difference could mean embedded images, revision history, or genuinely more content, and no diff at the byte level distinguishes those. Deferring them to a hand comparison — rather than applying either machine-wins rule — is the correct call, and the same restraint applied when the two-writer SecondBrain briefing conflict was resolved by staging both sides rather than adjudicating on the spot.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-worktree-gotchas]] — CRLF normalization as the mechanism inflating the conflict count.\n- [[connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine]] — CRLF, backslash paths, and `core.symlinks=false` persisting past the Windows machine; this is a further instance.\n- [[concepts/error-log-archive-collision-overwrite]] — The same-day sibling: a careless resolution destroying append-only history, recovered from git by luck.\n- [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]] — The consolidation effort that surfaced the conflict list.\n- [[concepts/cfb-victory-model-evaluation]] — The project whose forecast log holds the 324 at-risk rows; the pregame freeze captures are the file's entire value.\n- [[connections/ground-truth-checks-must-match-the-lying-layer]] — Identifying the transforming layer (CRLF) before deciding what the difference means.\n- [[connections/step-down-to-ground-truth-when-the-surface-lies]] — A conflict flag is a surface signal; the diff is the ground truth beneath it.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-09.md]] — GitHub consolidation session (22:06): 36 flagged Windows/Mac conflict files diffed down to 4 real ones — 26 CRLF-only, 6 `graphify-out/`/`portfolio.json` where Mac is newer — with the 32 safe files queued for deletion; `CFB_Victory_Model/data/weekly_forecast_log.csv` identified as an append-only log where the Mac-wins rule kept the smaller file and 324 Windows-only rows back to 2026-08-26 exist nowhere else; merge offered but not yet performed; three `SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework/deliverables/` Word/Excalidraw files left for hand comparison with the DRAFT at 1.0 MB on Windows vs 0.4 MB on Mac.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/machine-restore-closeout-handoff.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Machine-Restore Closeout and Handoff Note\"\naliases: [restore closeout, 2026-08-06-restore-closeout, handoff note, global-memory handoffs, restore checklist, parked items, compiler backlog 54 logs, zotero MCP registration]\ntags: [workflow, machine-restore, handoff, second-brain, claude-code, project-management]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-11\nupdated: 2026-08-11\n---\n\n# Machine-Restore Closeout and Handoff Note\n\nThe 2026-08-11 end-of-day session closed out a multi-day machine-restore checklist: an SSD backup baseline, a Zotero CLI/MCP bundle installation, cleanup of a failed-run artifact, and a written handoff note committed to the global-memory repository. The closeout distinguishes between items genuinely finished, items intentionally left to drain on their own schedule, and items deliberately parked.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Handoff artifact:** `~/.claude/global-memory/handoffs/2026-08-06-restore-closeout.md`, committed and pushed — a durable, versioned record of restore state rather than a memory-tier entry.\n- **Zotero MCP registered user-scope:** binary at `~/go/bin/zotero-research-library-pp-mcp`, registered as `zotero`; bundle at `~/printing-press/library/zotero-research-library/zotero-research-library.mcpb`.\n- **Backup baseline closed:** first full `CRASHBOX` run completed clean (OK, 0 errors, 21:25).\n- **Self-draining items are not blockers.** The compiler backlog of 54 daily logs drains on the nightly schedule; PR monitors for #1667 and #1668 were intentionally left stopped because the ball is on the maintainer's side.\n- **Parked items carried forward unchanged:** `GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD`, Milvus, and skill-tax — the same three items parked during the 2026-08-06 loose-ends sweep, confirming they remain deliberate deferrals rather than forgotten work.\n\n## Details\n\nThe closeout illustrates a three-way classification that keeps an end-of-day review honest. The first class is genuinely complete work with a verifiable artifact: the backup ran clean and produced a receipt, the MCP binary was registered and its bundle staged, and the handoff note was committed and pushed. The second class is work that is neither done nor blocking because a scheduled process will drain it without attention — the 54-log compiler backlog is the canonical example, since the nightly compile job consumes it incrementally, so listing it as an action item would misrepresent it as requiring human effort. The third class is work owned by someone else or explicitly deferred: PRs #1667 and #1668 sit with the maintainer, so the background monitors watching them were stopped rather than left running to generate noise.\n\nThe parked trio — `GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD`, Milvus, and skill-tax — is notable for having been carried unchanged since the 2026-08-06 sweep. Restating them at closeout rather than dropping them preserves the distinction between a deliberate deferral and an item that quietly fell off the list; a parked item that stops appearing in closeouts becomes indistinguishable from forgotten work. Routing the whole picture into a committed handoff file under `~/.claude/global-memory/handoffs/` rather than into a memory tier reflects the same routing discipline documented elsewhere: durable state that already has a natural on-disk home should live there, with memory reserved for pointers and conventions. Stopping the PR monitors also aligns with the finding that background monitor events are unreliable — a monitor left running against work that cannot progress produces duplicate or spurious notifications rather than signal.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline]] — The backup leg of the same closeout; its clean first baseline was one of the three completion criteria.\n- [[concepts/launchd-backup-receipt-race-repo-reconciliation]] — The 2026-08-06 loose-ends sweep that originally parked `GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD`, Milvus, and skill-tax and left repos pending reconciliation.\n- [[concepts/background-monitor-signal-reliability]] — Rationale for leaving the #1667/#1668 monitors stopped rather than running them against maintainer-owned work.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — The content-to-tier routing logic that puts a committed handoff file on disk rather than duplicating restore state into a memory tier.\n- [[concepts/zotero-research-library-pp-cli]] — The Zotero CLI whose MCP binary and `.mcpb` bundle were installed and registered during this closeout.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-11.md]] — Restore-closeout session (18:04): handoff note committed and pushed, Zotero MCP binary registered user-scope, backup baseline completed clean, PR monitors left stopped, compiler backlog and parked items carried forward.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Stack Doctor Sweep (2026-08-11): Self-Inflicted Repo Divergence and a Lying Dry Run\"\naliases: [claude-stack-doctor sweep, repo-diverged skills, skill-tax false positive, daily-backup.ps1 dry-run bug, MEMORY.md 100 percent, SecondBrain two-writer conflict, flush-error version skew 0.2.136]\ntags: [claude-code, automation, maintenance, git, memory, powershell, gotcha]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-01\nupdated: 2026-09-01\n---\n\n# Stack Doctor Sweep (2026-08-11): Self-Inflicted Repo Divergence and a Lying Dry Run\n\nA `/claude-stack-doctor` run on 2026-08-11 after a three-day gap took the automation/memory stack from 8 issues to 6 (three of the remaining six being lagging indicators that clear on the next nightly backup). The sweep's most valuable findings were not the fixes themselves but three cases where the *monitoring layer* was the defect: a nightly backup that committed its own generated files and so diverged a repo every night, a `skill-tax` check measuring the wrong quantity, and a `-DryRun` flag that reported git operations it never performed.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **`repo-diverged:skills` was self-inflicted.** Six tracked files were regenerated every run (`last-weekly-run.log` plus five `__pycache__` entries), so the nightly backup committed churn and diverged the repo nightly. Untracking and gitignoring them was the sweep's highest-leverage fix.\n- **`daily-backup.ps1 -DryRun` was lying.** The `Log` calls at lines 28/38 sat *outside* the `if (-not $DryRun)` guards at 24/34, so a dry run printed \"committed / pushed\" for git operations it never ran — briefly fooling the sweep itself. Fixed to `[dry-run] would commit`.\n- **`skill-tax` was a false positive from the monitor's own logic:** it counted all 133 on-disk `SKILL.md` files with no knowledge of `skillOverrides`. Verified empirically first (0 of 72 overridden skills leak into the listing; 61 expected, 60 seen), *then* the script was patched — the two candidate fixes were opposite, so the discriminating check had to precede either.\n- **`flush-error` recurrence across 8/08 and 8/11 indicated version skew, not contention:** `claude-agent-sdk` was 26 versions behind (0.2.110 → 0.2.136); upgrading restored flushes, and the memsearch flush patch was confirmed to survive the package operation.\n- **Both sides preserved on a two-writer conflict.** Four SecondBrain morning briefings differed from committed origin versions; origin's won by default and are live, with local copies staged at `doctor-backup-20260807-135924/secondbrain-local-briefings/*.local.md` — fully reversible rather than adjudicated on the spot.\n\n## Details\n\nThe three monitoring-layer defects share a structure worth naming: each produced a confident, well-formatted signal about a system state it was not actually measuring. The dry-run bug is the purest form — logging placed outside the guard it describes means the flag's entire purpose (previewing without acting) is silently void, and the operator's trust in the preview is inversely related to its accuracy. `skill-tax` is the same failure one level up: the check counted files on disk, a proxy that was correct before `skillOverrides` existed and became wrong afterward, so a previously-completed cleanup appeared not to have landed. The correct response in that case was explicitly *not* to fix either candidate immediately, because the two possible remedies (clean up more skills, or patch the counter) pointed in opposite directions; running the discriminating check first — do overridden skills leak into the listing? — decided which. The `repo-diverged:skills` case is the automation eating its own output: a backup job that commits generated artifacts guarantees divergence on every run, making a real alarm fire nightly for a cause the alarm cannot describe.\n\nThe sweep's restraint is as instructive as its fixes. Several issues were deliberately left alone with reasons recorded: 73 unversioned skill files (a design fork — adding `~/.agents/skills` to `backup-manifest.json` was proposed but not applied), unmanaged `DEV` directories (the user's scope call), a 4-log compiler backlog (self-healing at 2/night against ~1/day, and forcing it costs real money), and `snapshot:sessions` (an rsync race against a live Codex file, not rot). Two items were escalated as genuinely not auto-fixable and left open: Outlook auth expired, so morning briefings were shipping with 0 email threads pending an interactive `reauth.sh` run, and Google Drive 154h stale, with files reaching the local `G:` cache but GoogleDriveFS not syncing. The `MEMORY.md` file was at 100% — the next write would have failed — and was consolidated to 93% by shortening wording while keeping all 16 threads, rather than dropping any. Finally, two `flush-error` session IDs were classified benign only after evidence (the IDs exist nowhere; the DEV slug holds 28 transcripts, 3 in the last 24h), on the explicit principle of not classifying by inference; and two self-corrections were made before reporting (an \"8 → 2\" claim against a raw verdict of 6, and a claim that the /doctor changes were unversioned when the nightly backup had in fact committed them).\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — The recurring health-check routine this sweep instantiates; that article's post-restart ALL CLEAR is the same tool run under different conditions.\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — The `claude-agent-sdk` version-skew failure mode that recurred here (0.2.110 → 0.2.136), matching the documented `uv`-upgrade remedy.\n- [[concepts/committed-symlink-repo-landmine]] — The `~/.claude/skills` `git pull` that destroyed 73 files on 8/07; this sweep verified 21/21 symlinks resolve and 154 `SKILL.md` reachable after its own pull.\n- [[concepts/skill-description-budget-ceiling]] — The skill-listing budget that `skill-tax` exists to monitor; the false positive was a measurement error against that same ceiling.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — Another case where a backup subsystem's status reporting was narrower or staler than the operational question being asked.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — The general pattern the dry-run and `skill-tax` findings both instantiate.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-11.md]] — Stack doctor sweep (backfilled 2026-08-29 15:02): 8 → 6 verdict, `repo-diverged:skills` traced to six self-generated tracked files, `skill-tax` false positive verified empirically before patching, `daily-backup.ps1 -DryRun` logging outside its guards, `claude-agent-sdk` 26-version skew, `MEMORY.md` 100% → 93% with all 16 threads intact, SecondBrain two-writer conflict resolved by staging both sides, deliberately-deferred items with reasons, two open user-actionable items (Outlook reauth, GoogleDriveFS), and two pre-report self-corrections. Handoff at `~/.claude/global-memory/handoffs/2026-08-11-stack-doctor-sweep.md`.\n\n```\n\n### connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: A Status Field Answers a Narrower Question Than the One Being Asked\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag\"\n  - \"concepts/background-monitor-signal-reliability\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-31.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-31\nupdated: 2026-09-01\n---\n\n# Connection: A Status Field Answers a Narrower Question Than the One Being Asked\n\n## The Connection\n\n`launchctl list` column 2 reports the exit code of the *last scheduled* invocation of a job. The question being asked of it is \"is this backup healthy?\" These are different questions, and on 2026-08-31 they diverged: the manifest was fixed at 10:31, the manual re-run exited clean, and the status field stayed red because no scheduled run had occurred since. The same divergence appears in the 2026-08-06 background-monitor findings, where a comment-count bump answered \"did the count change?\" while being read as \"is there a new finding?\" — and counted the agent's own replies toward the total.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nThese are not cases of a signal being *wrong*. Each field reports accurately on its own narrow predicate; the failure is that the predicate is narrower than the operational question, and nothing at the display layer marks the gap. `launchctl` never claimed to know about out-of-band runs. A comment counter never claimed to distinguish authorship. The reader supplies the broader interpretation, and it holds right up until an event occurs that the field's scope excludes — a manual re-run, a self-authored reply, a duplicate delivery during a lockout.\n\nThis yields a specific diagnostic move, distinct from the general \"verify at ground truth\" rule: before reconciling a suspicious signal against an artifact, first ask what proposition the signal actually asserts. Doing so often dissolves the anomaly without any investigation — the 2026-08-31 red status needed no debugging once its scope was named, and the correct action became *leave it alone*, because the field will self-correct at the next scheduled tick. Forcing a manual re-run to turn it green would have been cosmetic, treating the display as the state. Where the earlier lessons say \"check the artifact when the layer lies,\" this one says \"check the layer's scope before deciding it lied at all.\"\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2026-08-31: \"`launchctl list` column 2 reflects the *last scheduled* run, not manual/out-of-band runs — a green backup log can coexist with a red launchd status. Confirm against the backup log timeline before treating the exit code as a live failure.\" The recommendation was explicitly to leave the stale code alone since it self-clears at the next 09:00 run.\n- 2026-08-06: background monitor comment-count bumps counted the agent's own replies as new findings — the counter answered \"count changed\" and was read as \"new external finding.\"\n- 2026-08-06: monitor events were silently dropped during a session-limit lockout (~15 duplicates, no dedup, backoff, or alert) — delivery status reported on transmission, not on receipt.\n- 2026-07-18: the SpeedProfiling background task's \"exit code 1\" reported on the process-management wrapper, not on whether the R script's work completed — the log showed a clean end-to-end run.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — The 2026-08-31 instance: a scheduled-run-scoped exit code read as current health.\n- [[concepts/background-monitor-signal-reliability]] — Comment-count and delivery signals whose scope excluded self-authorship and lockout drops.\n- [[connections/background-task-exit-code-false-failure]] — The neighbouring rule for when a signal genuinely misreports: reconcile against on-disk artifacts.\n- [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] — The parent discipline; this connection refines it by inserting a scope check before the verification step.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Claude Code Three-Tier Memory Architecture\"\naliases: [three-tier memory, Tier A memory, Tier B scratchpad, Stop hook per-turn, memory tiers, claude memory layers, hook firing mechanics, Tier B near cap, project memory files, memsearch, user_career_timeline, memory routing matrix, what not to store in memory]\ntags: [claude-code, memory, automation, hooks, infrastructure, architecture]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-05-19.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-05.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-18.md\"\ncreated: 2026-05-19\nupdated: 2026-08-07\n---\n\n# Claude Code Three-Tier Memory Architecture\n\nThe Claude Code memory system is composed of three independent layers, each operating at a different granularity and owned by a different mechanism. This architecture was articulated on 2026-05-19 during a session that traced exactly when and how each capture mechanism fires. On 2026-06-05, a practical capacity-management routing pattern emerged for when Tier B global memory approaches its character cap. On 2026-06-18, a worked example clarified how to route a specific piece of context across the tiers — and which content should not enter the memory system at all.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Tier A — Native Claude Code auto-memory:** Built into Claude Code itself; managed per-project; no user configuration required. Operates automatically within a project context; its exact retention and synthesis mechanics are not user-visible.\n- **Tier B — Custom global scratchpad via Stop hook:** A user-configured hook on the `Stop` event, which fires after every conversation turn. This provides incremental, per-turn transcript capture to a global scratchpad file (MEMORY.md) — a granularity that neither Tier A nor Tier C provides.\n- **Tier C — claude-memory-compiler:** The [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] system: `SessionEnd` and `PreCompact` hooks fire shell commands at session teardown or auto-compaction, spawning a detached `flush.py` process that appends structured notes to the daily log; at end-of-day these are compiled into knowledge articles.\n- **The agent never sees a \"session ending\" event.** Session end and compaction are harness-level events; it is the Claude Code harness that runs the hook shell commands, not the agent itself.\n- **PreCompact as a safety net:** In a long session with multiple auto-compactions, PreCompact fires before each one, preventing total context loss if the session later crashes before `SessionEnd`.\n- **When Tier B is near cap, route to Tier A project files:** The global MEMORY.md (Tier B) has a character-cap constraint. When it approaches capacity, new durable facts should be saved to Tier A project-specific memory files (e.g., `project_<name>.md`, `user_career_timeline.md`), which are cross-project searchable via memsearch.\n- **Content-to-tier routing matrix (2026-06-18):** file locations and versioning decisions belong in Tier B as an active-thread entry (\"continue this next session\"); durable personal conventions (e.g. \"always save v1 before editing\") belong in Tier A as a feedback rule; and the actual artifact content (slides, scripts) should NOT be duplicated in memory at all because it already lives on disk. Tier C's daily-log capture provides a compressed \"what did I do that day\" summary but is not a substitute for a durable artifact reference.\n\n## Details\n\nThe three tiers are designed to capture context at different time horizons and granularities, and they operate independently rather than hierarchically. Tier A is fully opaque to the user. Tier B's Stop-hook scratchpad is the most granular: because the Stop event fires after every conversation turn, Tier B can capture mid-session context incrementally in near-real-time. Tier C produces the richest synthesized output — compiled knowledge articles from daily logs — but fires only at session boundaries.\n\nThe independence of the three tiers is their key reliability property. A failure in one does not cascade to the others: if a session crashes before `SessionEnd` fires, the Stop-hook's Tier B scratchpad may still contain the session's important turns. The \"agent never sees session ending\" detail is architecturally important: code written inside the agent context cannot detect or react to its own session ending. Teardown logic must be placed in hook shell commands that the harness executes.\n\nThe Tier B near-cap routing pattern (2026-06-05) addresses a practical capacity constraint. The correct response when Tier B is near capacity is not to compress existing entries — which risks discarding information encoded in current phrasing — but to route new durable facts into named Tier A project memory files, which are surfaced by memsearch across all project contexts.\n\nThe 2026-06-18 worked example makes the routing decision concrete and, importantly, identifies content that should stay out of memory entirely. Asked how best to preserve a working session's context (an RTP presentation's file locations, a v1/v2 versioning decision, and the slides/script themselves), the assistant produced a routing matrix rather than dumping everything into one tier: the file paths and versioning state map to Tier B because they describe an active thread to resume next session; a general preference like \"always save v1 before editing\" maps to Tier A as a durable feedback rule; and the actual slide and script content maps to neither — it already exists as files on disk, so copying it into memory would only create a stale duplicate. The distinction matters because the memory system's value is in cheap-to-retrieve pointers and rules, not in re-storing artifacts that a file path already addresses. The same example noted that Tier C's automatic daily-log capture is a lossy summary (\"good for what did I do that day, not great for pick up exactly where I left off\"), reinforcing that a deliberate Tier B pointer is the right tool when the goal is precise session continuity.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — Detailed installation and configuration guide for Tier C.\n- [[concepts/daily-onramp-automation]] — A downstream consumer of Tier C's compiled daily-log output.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] — Maintenance pass on the same Claude Code environment that houses all three memory tiers.\n- [[concepts/end-of-day-structured-interview]] — Documents the USER.md 1,375-char cap and the Tier A vs. Tier B (durable vs. active) distinction.\n- [[concepts/weekly-review-synthesis-workflow]] — The end-of-week close-out workflow where near-cap Tier B → Tier A routing was first observed in practice.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-05-19.md]] — Three-tier architecture explanation, hook firing order, and \"agent never sees session ending\" clarification (16:53).\n- [[daily/2026-06-05.md]] — Tier B near-cap → Tier A project files routing pattern observed during end-of-week memory housekeeping (18:21).\n- [[daily/2026-06-18.md]] — Content-to-tier routing worked example (01:29): file locations/versioning → Tier B active thread, \"always save v1\" → Tier A feedback rule, actual artifact content → left on disk, not duplicated in memory.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"CRASHBOX SSD Backup Baseline and Mid-Run Volume Rename\"\naliases: [CRASHBOX, SSD backup baseline, AssistantBackup, backup manifest path, volume rename mid-backup, rsync incremental resume, diskutil rename, local-only backup, Drive upload deferral]\ntags: [backup, macos, storage, automation, rsync, workflow, machine-restore]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-11\nupdated: 2026-08-11\n---\n\n# CRASHBOX SSD Backup Baseline and Mid-Run Volume Rename\n\nOn 2026-08-11, as part of a machine-restore checklist closeout, an external SSD was renamed to `CRASHBOX` and used to capture the first full local backup baseline at `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup`. The rename occurred while a backup run was already in flight; the run was restarted against the new path and completed clean (OK, 0 errors) at 21:25. The session also established which parts of the backup pipeline require network connectivity and which do not.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **First full CRASHBOX baseline completed clean:** OK with 0 errors at 21:25, after the backup manifest was repointed to `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup` and the in-flight run restarted.\n- **Mid-backup volume rename is safe.** Already-copied data survives the rename, and rsync's incremental behavior means a restarted run — or one interrupted by power loss — resumes rather than redoing completed work.\n- **Volume names containing spaces work fine in the PowerShell backup manifest;** the rename to `CRASHBOX` was cosmetic, not a correctness fix.\n- **Network dependency is confined to one step.** The SSD copy is fully local; Google Drive writes cache locally and upload later; only the git commit/push step needs the network, and it self-heals on the next nightly run. The machine could therefore be safely disconnected and shut down mid-pipeline.\n- **The rename was performed via a one-off manual-mode approval** rather than a standing `Bash(diskutil rename:*)` allow rule, since no recurrence was expected.\n\n## Details\n\nThe backup pipeline has three write targets with different durability and connectivity characteristics, and the session made this stratification explicit. The local SSD copy is synchronous and offline-capable. The Google Drive leg writes to a locally-cached CloudStorage path and defers the actual upload, so it neither blocks nor requires connectivity at run time — the same local-cache-then-upload behavior that previously caused a receipt read-back race when a job read a freshly-written receipt before Drive had flushed it. Only the git commit and push step genuinely needs the network, and because the backup job runs nightly, a failed push is self-healing: the next run retries it. This meant the correct answer to \"do I need internet before shutting down?\" was no, with a single deferred and self-recovering exception.\n\nThe mid-run rename was the session's operationally interesting move. Renaming a mounted volume changes the `/Volumes/<name>` mount path, which invalidates any manifest entry or in-progress run referencing the old path — but it does not touch the copied files themselves. Because the backup uses rsync semantics, restarting against the corrected path performs an incremental pass over the already-transferred data rather than a full recopy, so the cost of the restart is proportional to what remained rather than to the total. This same property makes the run resilient to power interruption, which matters for a first full baseline that transfers the largest volume of data it ever will. The rename itself was cosmetic; the manifest tolerated the prior name, spaces included.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/launchd-backup-receipt-race-repo-reconciliation]] — The same backup subsystem, where a Google Drive CloudStorage write-lag raced a receipt read-back and the job was moved under `launchd`; this article records the local-SSD leg and its offline-capable characteristics.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-permission-modes]] — The rename was gated by the permission system and resolved via a one-off manual-mode approval rather than a standing allow rule.\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — The broader scheduled-automation stack (nightly backup task, reindex, cloud checks) that this baseline run belongs to.\n- [[concepts/in-session-background-process-limits]] — Explains why durable, long-running backup work belongs in an OS-scheduled job rather than an in-session background command.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-11.md]] — Restore-closeout session (18:04): SSD renamed to `CRASHBOX`, manifest repointed to `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup`, in-flight run restarted, network-dependency analysis (local SSD / deferred Drive upload / self-healing git push), first full baseline completed OK with 0 errors at 21:25.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/custom-claude-code-skills.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Custom Claude Code Skills: web-scraping, ingest-resource, improve-system\"\naliases: [web-scraping skill, ingest-resource skill, improve-system skill, write-a-skill process, SecondBrain vault]\ntags: [claude-code, skills, automation, second-brain, web-scraping]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-10.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-04-21.md\"\ncreated: 2026-06-10\nupdated: 2026-06-15\n---\n\n# Custom Claude Code Skills: web-scraping, ingest-resource, improve-system\n\nThree custom Claude Code skills - `/web-scraping`, `/ingest-resource`, and `/improve-system` - were built in a single session on 2026-06-10 using the standard \"write-a-skill\" process, and registered under `~\\.claude\\skills\\`. The vault-facing skills were tailored to the user's actual SecondBrain vault structure rather than a generic structure inferred from prompt screenshots.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- `/web-scraping` routes by source type: free WebFetch first for static pages, Exa for semantic/discovery search, and Firecrawl v2 for JS-heavy/SPA pages; implemented as stdlib-only Python scripts (`exa_search.py`, `firecrawl_scrape.py`) that compile and fail cleanly when API keys are missing.\n- `/ingest-resource` performs tiered acquisition (WebFetch, then falling back to web-scraping's Firecrawl script) and routes content by \"shelf life\": stable reference material goes to a new `Knowledge/` folder, active-work material goes to the existing `Projects/` folder, and `Notes/` (interview-me territory) and `Inbox/` are left untouched.\n- `/improve-system` offers 5 modes - Audit, Skill Review, Experience, Historical Review, and Foundation - selected via signal-phrase routing; Audit mode archives rather than deletes and reports before touching anything, while Foundation mode hands off to `starter-voice-profile`, `starter-icp-positioning`, and `starter-design-tokens` skills.\n- Both vault-facing skills were adapted to the real SecondBrain vault at `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\`, including its existing frontmatter conventions and wikilink style.\n- Verified API contracts: Exa uses `POST /search` with an `x-api-key` header; Firecrawl v2 uses `POST /v2/scrape` with Bearer auth. Real API keys still need to be added to `~\\.claude\\skills\\web-scraping\\.env` (currently commented placeholders).\n\n## Details\n\nThe three skills were designed to compose with each other rather than operate in isolation: `/ingest-resource` calls `/web-scraping`'s Firecrawl script as a fallback when WebFetch alone can't retrieve a page, and `/improve-system` delegates to the `starter-*` skills for its Foundation mode rather than reimplementing that logic. This composition-first design means improvements to one skill (e.g., a new scraping technique added to `/web-scraping`) can propagate to the others automatically.\n\nNewly written skills register immediately in the available-skills list as long as their frontmatter is valid - the user confirmed this by running `/reload-skills` and `/reload-plugins` after writing all three. The remaining open task is operational rather than design-related: the `.env` file for `/web-scraping` still contains commented placeholder keys for Exa and Firecrawl, and a live smoke test of both APIs was suggested once real keys are added.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] - Skill creation followed the same day's plugin/skill registration maintenance.\n- [[concepts/catapult-connect-api-reference]] - A scraping technique discovered later that day (the ReadMe `.md`-suffix trick) is a candidate addition to `/web-scraping` and `/ingest-resource`'s routing logic.\n- [[connections/web-scraping-skill-and-readme-doc-trick]] - Explores the gap between this skill's routing tiers and the technique that actually worked for Catapult's documentation site.\n- [[connections/obsidian-vault-as-knowledge-hub]] - Connects this session's SecondBrain vault-facing skills to an earlier (2026-04-21) decision to treat an Obsidian vault as the destination for graphify's research-synthesis output from SportScienceResearchRepo.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-10.md]] - Skill design, API verification, and registration session.\n- [[daily/2026-04-21.md]] - Source of the earlier (2026-04-21) Obsidian-vault-as-destination decision referenced in [[connections/obsidian-vault-as-knowledge-hub]].\n\n```\n\n### concepts/error-log-archive-collision-overwrite.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Archive Filename Collision Overwrote Prior .errors.log Entries\"\naliases: [.errors.log.archived-2026-09-09 collision, cp overwrote archive, date-keyed archive filename, append not copy, cp /dev/null truncation, truncate not installed, dcg blocks HOME shell truncation]\ntags: [automation, maintenance, macos, gotcha, verification, git]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-09.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-09\nupdated: 2026-09-09\n---\n\n# Archive Filename Collision Overwrote Prior .errors.log Entries\n\nClearing `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log` on 2026-09-09 destroyed data: a `cp` to the date-keyed archive path `.errors.log.archived-2026-09-09` overwrote three earlier error lines from 9/8–9/9 that a previous archive operation had already written there. The lines were recovered from git. The fix direction identified was that archive filenames must not collide, or the archive step must append rather than copy.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **A date-keyed archive filename is a collision hazard whenever the operation can run twice in one day.** The second archive silently replaces the first, and `cp` gives no indication that it destroyed content.\n- **The archive step should append, not copy** — or the filename should carry enough resolution (timestamp, run ID) to be unique per invocation.\n- **Recovery came from git**, which held the prior archive contents; version control was the only backstop for a destructive step in the very tooling meant to preserve failure history.\n- **A successful backup run does not clear a prior failure's `.errors.log` entry.** The log is append-only and must be archived and emptied manually.\n- **Emptying required `cp /dev/null`** because shell redirection truncation under `$HOME` is blocked by a dcg rule and `truncate` is not installed on the machine.\n\n## Details\n\nThis is the second time a shared date-keyed output path has destroyed prior content in this stack. The first was memlint's free structural run overwriting a paid LLM report, documented in [[concepts/free-run-overwrites-paid-report]], where the remedy was to give each mode its own output path. The shape is identical: a filename derived solely from the date is unique only if the operation is genuinely once-daily, and neither of these operations is — a lint can be run in both modes on the same day, and an error log can need archiving more than once on a day with multiple failures. The `cp` verb compounds it, because copy-to-existing is a silent replace whereas an append (`>>` or `cat >>`) would have preserved both sets of lines with no filename change at all. Appending is the better fix here specifically because the archive is a history file: its purpose is accumulation, and a per-run unique filename merely scatters that history across many files.\n\nThe irony is worth naming, because it is the operative lesson rather than a stylistic observation. `.errors.log` exists as an asynchronous escalation channel for scheduled jobs that have no session to report to, and the discipline documented in [[concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline]] is to archive before clearing precisely so that a wrong staleness call remains recoverable. The archive step is therefore the safety mechanism, and here it was the thing that lost the data. Any procedure whose whole justification is \"preserve before destroying\" must be verified to actually preserve; a preservation step that silently overwrites is worse than no preservation step, because it induces confidence in a discarded recovery path. That the lines were recoverable from git is fortunate rather than designed — it was a second, incidental copy, not the archive doing its job.\n\nThe tooling constraints around emptying the file are a smaller but reusable finding. Shell truncation via redirection under `$HOME` is blocked by a dcg rule, which is the same guard class that has repeatedly produced false positives on home-directory writes and pushed work toward native file tools per [[concepts/dcg-guard-false-positives-native-tool-workaround]]. With `truncate` also unavailable, `cp /dev/null <file>` is the working route on this machine — a useful entry to have recorded, since the obvious two options both fail for unrelated reasons.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline]] — Establishes the archive-then-clear procedure whose archive step failed here; that article's guarantee depends on this defect being fixed.\n- [[concepts/free-run-overwrites-paid-report]] — The prior instance of a shared date-keyed filename destroying content, and the per-mode-path remedy.\n- [[concepts/dcg-guard-false-positives-native-tool-workaround]] — The guard that blocked shell truncation under `$HOME`, forcing the `cp /dev/null` workaround.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — The same session; the error entries being archived came from that run's manifest and push failures.\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — The routine that reads `.errors.log` at session start and depends on it being an accurate, non-stale signal.\n- [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]] — The `/backup` skill automates the archive-then-clear step, making this collision a defect in automated tooling rather than an ad-hoc slip.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-09.md]] — Backup repair session (19:17): `cp` to `.errors.log.archived-2026-09-09` overwrote three earlier error lines from 9/8–9/9, recovered from git; noted that archive filenames need to not collide or the copy needs to append; a successful run does not clear a prior failure's entry, requiring manual archive and `cp /dev/null` emptying because shell truncation under `$HOME` is dcg-blocked and `truncate` is not installed.\n\n```\n\nThese are the 13 articles (of 294) most related to this log, plus graph neighbours and least-linked articles. This is the relevant context, not a partial view to be cautious about.\n- The Current Wiki Index above lists EVERY article. Consult it before creating a new one, and you may [[wikilink]] any article listed there even if its text is not shown.\n- If this log adds detail, correction, or a new example to an article shown above, UPDATE it: re-emit it at its existing path with the full revised content. Updating existing articles is a primary expected outcome, not a fallback.\n\n## Daily Log to Compile\n\n**File:** 2026-09-24.md\n\n# Daily Log: 2026-09-24\n\n## Sessions\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Memory Flush (19:47)\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n### Session (21:25)\n\n**Context:** End-of-day Mac work on KeystoneOS — finishing the \"Windows = day machine, Mac = evening machine\" split, then running `/closeout`.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Confirmed no installer script rewrites the `com.keystone.dailybackup.plist` schedule, so a hand edit is safe.\n- User asked twice to \"finish the Mac half\"; verification showed it was already complete, confirmed by the 21:12 backup run.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Picked KeystoneOS as the active project: the 13:20 merge group was the newest real work; CFB's later commits were only sync commits, not work.\n- Turned off `com.keystone.rituals` on the Mac (persists after reboot) — rituals nudges now come only from Windows.\n- Moved Mac `daily-backup` from 09:00 to 21:00 to match the evening-machine role; kept a backup of the old plist.\n- On the two clashing transcript logs (9/23, 9/24), kept text from both sides by deleting only the conflict markers.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- The 9/23 and 9/24 `daily-backup` failures were `repo-behind-blocked:global-memory` — the nightly push cannot run while the repo is behind GitHub. Today's pull fixed it; the 21:12 run pushed cleanly.\n- A newer commit timestamp does not mean newer work. Sync commits masked the real most-recent project.\n- A push can fail from a dropped network mid-command; a plain retry worked.\n\n**Action Items:**\n1. On Windows: confirm the `KeystoneOS-Rituals` scheduled task is enabled.\n2. Add `RunningTheRoom` to `backup-manifest.json` (backup log reports `manifest-unmanaged:RunningTheRoom`).\n3. Run `compile.py` by hand — memory compiler is 3 logs behind.\n4. GPS audit build 2 clean-up: delete 3 old copies by hand.\n5. Plug in the CRASHBOX drive before 21:00 if the SSD copy is wanted.\n\n**Files of record:**\n- `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/handoffs/2026-09-24-mac-evening-split.md`\n- `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/STATUS.md`\n- `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.dailybackup.plist` (+ `.bak-2026-09-24`)\n\n\n\n## Your Task\n\nDo NOT use any tools and do NOT attempt to write files — your sandbox cannot\nwrite under this directory. Instead, emit the articles as plain text using the\nexact delimiter format below. The orchestrator parses your output and writes the\nfiles itself.\n\n### Output format\n\nFor each new or updated article, emit a block:\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/<slug>.md===\n<full markdown: complete YAML frontmatter + body, per the AGENTS.md schema>\n===END ARTICLE===\n\nUse `connections/<slug>.md` for connection articles and `qa/<slug>.md` for Q&A.\nSlugs are lowercase and hyphenated. To UPDATE an existing article, emit it with\nits existing path and the full revised content (it overwrites).\n\nAfter the article blocks, emit exactly one index block and one log block:\n\n===INDEX===\n| [[concepts/<slug>]] | one-line summary | 2026-09-24.md | 2026-09-24 |\n===END INDEX===\n\n===LOG===\n## [2026-09-24T21:30:18-05:00] compile | 2026-09-24.md\n- Source: daily/2026-09-24.md\n- Articles created: [[concepts/<slug>]]\n- Articles updated: (none)\n===END LOG===\n\n### Rules\n1. Identify 3-7 distinct concepts worth their own article.\n2. Emit ===NOTHING=== ONLY when the log itself contains no durable knowledge\n   (e.g. \"FLUSH_OK\", or purely transient chatter). \"An article already covers\n   this topic\" is NOT a reason to emit ===NOTHING=== - it is a reason to UPDATE\n   that article with whatever this log adds.\n3. Every article: complete YAML frontmatter (title, sources, created, updated)\n   with `sources` citing 2026-09-24.md; at least 2 [[wikilinks]]; Key Points\n   3-5 bullets; Details 2+ paragraphs; Related Concepts 2+ entries; encyclopedia\n   style — neutral and comprehensive. Follow the AGENTS.md article format exactly.\n4. Create a connection article when the log reveals a non-obvious relationship\n   between 2+ concepts.\n4b. Do NOT re-emit an article whose only change would be the `updated:` date or\n   a restatement of what it already says. Measured on a real run, 4 of 5\n   \"updates\" changed nothing but the timestamp - that is churn: it rewrites\n   files, dirties backups, and invalidates caches while adding no knowledge.\n   Re-emit an article only when you are adding substantive content.\n5. Add one ===INDEX=== table row per article (new or updated).\n\nEmit ONLY the delimiter blocks — no preamble, no commentary.\n"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"dequeue","timestamp":"2026-09-25T02:30:22.621Z","sessionId":"3b9a07b6-09e3-452b-9767-d2170563b3d9"}
{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":false,"promptId":"166ff1a7-bedb-4ca4-a2b6-05af4d61d5bd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"You are a knowledge compiler. Your job is to read a daily conversation log\nand extract knowledge into structured wiki articles.\n\n## Schema (AGENTS.md)\n\n# AGENTS.md - Personal Knowledge Base Schema\n\n> Adapted from [Andrej Karpathy's LLM Knowledge Base](https://gist.github.com/karpathy/442a6bf555914893e9891c11519de94f) architecture.\n> Instead of ingesting external articles, this system compiles knowledge from your own AI conversations.\n\n## The Compiler Analogy\n\n```\ndaily/          = source code    (your conversations - the raw material)\nLLM             = compiler       (extracts and organizes knowledge)\nknowledge/      = executable     (structured, queryable knowledge base)\nlint            = test suite     (health checks for consistency)\nmemlint         = test suite     (same, for the memory files loaded each session)\nqueries         = runtime        (using the knowledge)\n```\n\nYou don't manually organize your knowledge. You have conversations, and the LLM handles the synthesis, cross-referencing, and maintenance.\n\n---\n\n## Architecture\n\n### Layer 1: `daily/` - Conversation Logs (Immutable Source)\n\nDaily logs capture what happened in your AI coding sessions. These are the \"raw sources\" - append-only, never edited after the fact.\n\n```\ndaily/\n├── 2026-04-01.md\n├── 2026-04-02.md\n├── ...\n```\n\nEach file follows this format:\n\n```markdown\n# Daily Log: YYYY-MM-DD\n\n## Sessions\n\n### Session (HH:MM) - Brief Title\n\n**Context:** What the user was working on.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- User asked about X, assistant explained Y\n- Decided to use Z approach because...\n- Discovered that W doesn't work when...\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Chose library X over Y because...\n- Architecture: went with pattern Z\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Always do X before Y to avoid...\n- The gotcha with Z is that...\n\n**Action Items:**\n- [ ] Follow up on X\n- [ ] Refactor Y when time permits\n```\n\n### Layer 2: `knowledge/` - Compiled Knowledge (LLM-Owned)\n\nThe LLM owns this directory entirely. Humans read it but rarely edit it directly.\n\n```\nknowledge/\n├── index.md              # Master catalog - every article with one-line summary\n├── log.md                # Append-only chronological build log\n├── concepts/             # Atomic knowledge articles\n├── connections/          # Cross-cutting insights linking 2+ concepts\n└── qa/                   # Filed query answers (compounding knowledge)\n```\n\n### Layer 3: This File (AGENTS.md)\n\nThe schema that tells the LLM how to compile and maintain the knowledge base. This is the \"compiler specification.\"\n\n---\n\n## Structural Files\n\n### `knowledge/index.md` - Master Catalog\n\nA table listing every knowledge article. This is the primary retrieval mechanism - the LLM reads this FIRST when answering any query, then selects relevant articles to read in full.\n\nFormat:\n\n```markdown\n# Knowledge Base Index\n\n| Article | Summary | Compiled From | Updated |\n|---------|---------|---------------|---------|\n| [[concepts/supabase-auth]] | Row-level security patterns and JWT gotchas | daily/2026-04-02.md | 2026-04-02 |\n| [[connections/auth-and-webhooks]] | Token verification patterns shared across Supabase auth and Stripe webhooks | daily/2026-04-02.md, daily/2026-04-04.md | 2026-04-04 |\n```\n\n### `knowledge/log.md` - Build Log\n\nAppend-only chronological record of every compile, query, and lint operation.\n\nFormat:\n\n```markdown\n# Build Log\n\n## [2026-04-01T14:30:00] compile | Daily Log 2026-04-01\n- Source: daily/2026-04-01.md\n- Articles created: [[concepts/nextjs-project-structure]], [[concepts/tailwind-setup]]\n- Articles updated: (none)\n\n## [2026-04-02T09:00:00] query | \"How do I handle auth redirects?\"\n- Consulted: [[concepts/supabase-auth]], [[concepts/nextjs-middleware]]\n- Filed to: [[qa/auth-redirect-handling]]\n```\n\n---\n\n## Article Formats\n\n### Concept Articles (`knowledge/concepts/`)\n\nOne article per atomic piece of knowledge. These are facts, patterns, decisions, preferences, and lessons extracted from your conversations.\n\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Concept Name\"\naliases: [alternate-name, abbreviation]\ntags: [domain, topic]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-01.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-04-03.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-01\nupdated: 2026-04-03\n---\n\n# Concept Name\n\n[2-4 sentence core explanation]\n\n## Key Points\n\n- [Bullet points, each self-contained]\n\n## Details\n\n[Deeper explanation, encyclopedia-style paragraphs]\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/related-concept]] - How it connects\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-04-01.md]] - Initial discovery during project setup\n- [[daily/2026-04-03.md]] - Updated after debugging session\n```\n\n### Connection Articles (`knowledge/connections/`)\n\nCross-cutting synthesis linking 2+ concepts. Created when a conversation reveals a non-obvious relationship.\n\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: X and Y\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/concept-x\"\n  - \"concepts/concept-y\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-04.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-04\nupdated: 2026-04-04\n---\n\n# Connection: X and Y\n\n## The Connection\n\n[What links these concepts]\n\n## Key Insight\n\n[The non-obvious relationship discovered]\n\n## Evidence\n\n[Specific examples from conversations]\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/concept-x]]\n- [[concepts/concept-y]]\n```\n\n### Q&A Articles (`knowledge/qa/`)\n\nFiled answers from queries. Every complex question answered by the system can be permanently stored, making future queries smarter.\n\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Q: Original Question\"\nquestion: \"The exact question asked\"\nconsulted:\n  - \"concepts/article-1\"\n  - \"concepts/article-2\"\nfiled: 2026-04-05\n---\n\n# Q: Original Question\n\n## Answer\n\n[The synthesized answer with [[wikilinks]] to sources]\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- [[concepts/article-1]] - Relevant because...\n- [[concepts/article-2]] - Provided context on...\n\n## Follow-Up Questions\n\n- What about edge case X?\n- How does this change if Y?\n```\n\n---\n\n## Core Operations\n\n### 1. Compile (daily/ -> knowledge/)\n\nWhen processing a daily log:\n\n1. Read the daily log file\n2. Read `knowledge/index.md` to understand current knowledge state\n3. Read existing articles that may need updating\n4. For each piece of knowledge found in the log:\n   - If an existing concept article covers this topic: UPDATE it with new information, add the daily log as a source\n   - If it's a new topic: CREATE a new `concepts/` article\n5. If the log reveals a non-obvious connection between 2+ existing concepts: CREATE a `connections/` article\n6. UPDATE `knowledge/index.md` with new/modified entries\n7. APPEND to `knowledge/log.md`\n\n**Important guidelines:**\n- A single daily log may touch 3-10 knowledge articles\n- Prefer updating existing articles over creating near-duplicates\n- Use Obsidian-style `[[wikilinks]]` with full relative paths from knowledge/\n- Write in encyclopedia style - factual, concise, self-contained\n- Every article must have YAML frontmatter\n- Every article must link back to its source daily logs\n\n### 2. Query (Ask the Knowledge Base)\n\n1. Read `knowledge/index.md` (the master catalog)\n2. Based on the question, identify 3-10 relevant articles from the index\n3. Read those articles in full\n4. Synthesize an answer with `[[wikilink]]` citations\n5. If `--file-back` is specified: create a `knowledge/qa/` article and update index.md and log.md\n\n**Why this works without RAG:** At personal knowledge base scale (50-500 articles), the LLM reading a structured index outperforms cosine similarity. The LLM understands what the question is really asking and selects pages accordingly. Embeddings find similar words; the LLM finds relevant concepts.\n\n### 3. Lint (Health Checks)\n\nSeven checks, run periodically:\n\n1. **Broken links** - `[[wikilinks]]` pointing to non-existent articles\n2. **Orphan pages** - Articles with zero inbound links from other articles\n3. **Orphan sources** - Daily logs that haven't been compiled yet\n4. **Stale articles** - Source daily log changed since article was last compiled\n5. **Contradictions** - Conflicting claims across articles (requires LLM judgment)\n6. **Missing backlinks** - A links to B but B doesn't link back to A\n7. **Sparse articles** - Below 200 words, likely incomplete\n\nOutput: a markdown report with severity levels (error, warning, suggestion).\n\n---\n\n## Conventions\n\n- **Wikilinks:** Use Obsidian-style `[[path/to/article]]` without `.md` extension\n- **Writing style:** Encyclopedia-style, factual, third-person where appropriate\n- **Dates:** ISO 8601 (YYYY-MM-DD for dates, full ISO for timestamps in log.md)\n- **File naming:** lowercase, hyphens for spaces (e.g., `supabase-row-level-security.md`)\n- **Frontmatter:** Every article must have YAML frontmatter with at minimum: title, sources, created, updated\n- **Sources:** Always link back to the daily log(s) that contributed to an article\n\n---\n\n## Full Project Structure\n\n```\nllm-personal-kb/\n|-- .claude/\n|   |-- settings.json                # Hook configuration (auto-activates in Claude Code)\n|-- .gitignore                       # Excludes runtime state, temp files, caches\n|-- AGENTS.md                        # This file - schema + full technical reference\n|-- README.md                        # Concise overview + quick start\n|-- pyproject.toml                   # Dependencies (at root so hooks can find it)\n|-- daily/                           # \"Source code\" - conversation logs (immutable)\n|-- knowledge/                       # \"Executable\" - compiled knowledge (LLM-owned)\n|   |-- index.md                     #   Master catalog - THE retrieval mechanism\n|   |-- log.md                       #   Append-only build log\n|   |-- concepts/                    #   Atomic knowledge articles\n|   |-- connections/                 #   Cross-cutting insights linking 2+ concepts\n|   |-- qa/                          #   Filed query answers (compounding knowledge)\n|-- scripts/                         # CLI tools\n|   |-- compile.py                   #   Compile daily logs -> knowledge articles\n|   |-- query.py                     #   Ask questions (index-guided, no RAG)\n|   |-- lint.py                      #   7 health checks (knowledge base)\n|   |-- memlint.py                   #   6 health checks (memory tier)\n|   |-- flush.py                     #   Extract memories from conversations (background)\n|   |-- config.py                    #   Path constants\n|   |-- utils.py                     #   Shared helpers\n|-- hooks/                           # Claude Code hooks\n|   |-- session-start.py             #   Injects knowledge into every session\n|   |-- session-end.py               #   Extracts conversation -> daily log\n|   |-- pre-compact.py               #   Safety net: captures context before compaction\n|-- reports/                         # Lint reports (gitignored)\n```\n\n---\n\n## Hook System (Automatic Capture)\n\nHooks are configured in `.claude/settings.json` and fire automatically when you use Claude Code in this project.\n\n### `.claude/settings.json` Format\n\n```json\n{\n  \"hooks\": {\n    \"SessionStart\": [{ \"matcher\": \"\", \"hooks\": [{ \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"uv run python hooks/session-start.py\", \"timeout\": 15 }] }],\n    \"PreCompact\": [{ \"matcher\": \"\", \"hooks\": [{ \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"uv run python hooks/pre-compact.py\", \"timeout\": 10 }] }],\n    \"SessionEnd\": [{ \"matcher\": \"\", \"hooks\": [{ \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"uv run python hooks/session-end.py\", \"timeout\": 10 }] }]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nCommands use simple relative paths from the project root. Empty `matcher` catches all events.\n\n### Hook Details\n\n**`session-start.py`** (SessionStart)\n- Pure local I/O, no API calls, runs in under 1 second\n- Reads `knowledge/index.md` and the most recent daily log\n- Outputs JSON to stdout: `{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"...\"}}`\n- Claude sees the knowledge base index at the start of every session\n- Max context: 20,000 characters\n\n**`session-end.py`** (SessionEnd)\n- Reads hook input from stdin (JSON with `session_id`, `transcript_path`, `cwd`)\n- Copies the raw JSONL transcript to a temp file (no parsing in the hook - keeps it fast)\n- Spawns `flush.py` as a fully detached background process\n- Recursion guard: exits immediately if `CLAUDE_INVOKED_BY` env var is set\n\n**`pre-compact.py`** (PreCompact)\n- Same architecture as session-end.py\n- Fires before Claude Code auto-compacts the context window\n- Guards against empty `transcript_path` (known Claude Code bug #13668)\n- Critical for long sessions: captures context before summarization discards it\n\n**Why both PreCompact and SessionEnd?** Long-running sessions may trigger multiple auto-compactions before you close the session. Without PreCompact, intermediate context is lost to summarization before SessionEnd ever fires.\n\n### Background Flush Process (`flush.py`)\n\nSpawned by both hooks as a fully detached background process:\n- **Windows:** `CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP | DETACHED_PROCESS` flags\n- **Mac/Linux:** `start_new_session=True`\n\nThis ensures flush.py survives after Claude Code's hook process exits.\n\n**What flush.py does:**\n1. Sets `CLAUDE_INVOKED_BY=memory_flush` env var (prevents recursive hook firing)\n2. Reads the pre-extracted conversation context from the temp `.md` file\n3. Skips if context is empty or if same session was flushed within 60 seconds (deduplication)\n4. Calls Claude Agent SDK (`query()` with `allowed_tools=[]`, `max_turns=2`)\n5. Claude decides what's worth saving - returns structured bullet points or `FLUSH_OK`\n6. Appends result to `daily/YYYY-MM-DD.md`\n7. Cleans up temp context file\n8. **End-of-day auto-compilation:** If it's past 6 PM local time (`COMPILE_AFTER_HOUR = 18`) and today's daily log has changed since its last compilation (hash comparison against `state.json`), spawns `compile.py` as another detached background process. This means compilation happens automatically once a day without needing a cron job or manual trigger.\n\n### JSONL Transcript Format\n\nClaude Code stores conversations as `.jsonl` files. Messages are nested under a `message` key:\n\n```python\nentry = json.loads(line)\nmsg = entry.get(\"message\", {})\nrole = msg.get(\"role\", \"\")     # \"user\" or \"assistant\"\ncontent = msg.get(\"content\", \"\")  # string or list of content blocks\n```\n\nContent can be a string or a list of blocks (`{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"...\"}` dicts).\n\n---\n\n## Script Details\n\n### compile.py - The Compiler\n\nUses the Claude Agent SDK's async streaming `query()`:\n\n```python\nasync for message in query(\n    prompt=compile_prompt,\n    options=ClaudeAgentOptions(\n        cwd=str(ROOT_DIR),\n        system_prompt={\"type\": \"preset\", \"preset\": \"claude_code\"},\n        allowed_tools=[\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Edit\", \"Glob\", \"Grep\"],\n        permission_mode=\"acceptEdits\",\n        max_turns=30,\n    ),\n):\n```\n\n- Builds a prompt with: AGENTS.md schema, current index, all existing articles, and the daily log\n- Claude reads the daily log, decides what concepts to extract, and writes files directly\n- `permission_mode=\"acceptEdits\"` auto-approves all file operations\n- Incremental: tracks SHA-256 hashes of daily logs in `state.json`, skips unchanged files\n- Cost: ~$0.45-0.65 per daily log (increases as KB grows)\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/compile.py              # compile new/changed only\nuv run python scripts/compile.py --all        # force recompile everything\nuv run python scripts/compile.py --file daily/2026-04-01.md\nuv run python scripts/compile.py --dry-run\n```\n\n### query.py - Index-Guided Retrieval\n\nLoads the entire knowledge base into context (index + all articles). No RAG.\n\nAt personal KB scale (50-500 articles), the LLM reading a structured index outperforms vector similarity. The LLM understands what you're really asking; cosine similarity just finds similar words.\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/query.py \"What auth patterns do I use?\"\nuv run python scripts/query.py \"What's my error handling strategy?\" --file-back\n```\n\nWith `--file-back`, creates a Q&A article in `knowledge/qa/` and updates the index and log. This is the compounding loop - every question makes the KB smarter.\n\n### lint.py - Health Checks\n\nSeven checks:\n\n| Check | Type | Catches |\n|-------|------|---------|\n| Broken links | Structural | `[[wikilinks]]` to non-existent articles |\n| Orphan pages | Structural | Articles with zero inbound links |\n| Orphan sources | Structural | Daily logs not yet compiled |\n| Stale articles | Structural | Source logs changed since compilation |\n| Missing backlinks | Structural | A links to B but B doesn't link back |\n| Sparse articles | Structural | Under 200 words |\n| Contradictions | LLM | Conflicting claims across articles |\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/lint.py                    # all checks\nuv run python scripts/lint.py --structural-only  # skip LLM check (free)\n```\n\nReports saved to `reports/lint-YYYY-MM-DD.md`.\n\n### memlint.py - Memory Tier Health Checks\n\n`lint.py` stops at `knowledge/`. The files the harness actually injects into every\nsession live elsewhere and were never audited: `~/.claude/global-memory/` and the\nactive `~/.claude/projects/*/memory/` slugs. Drift there is the most expensive\ndrift in the system, because it is read at the start of every conversation.\n\nSeven checks:\n\n| Check | Type | Catches |\n|-------|------|---------|\n| Index drift | Structural | Memory file missing from MEMORY.md, or an index pointer with no file |\n| Broken links | Structural | `[[links]]` that do not match any frontmatter `name:` in the slug |\n| Name form | Structural | `name:` that is not the bare kebab stem (type belongs in `metadata.type`) |\n| Near-duplicates | Structural | Within-slug TF-IDF similarity above the threshold |\n| Cap overruns | Structural | A file past the character cap it declares in its own header |\n| Content in index | Structural | Facts written into MEMORY.md, which is meant to hold pointers |\n| Contradictions | LLM | Conflicting, redundant, or superseded claims across memory files |\n\n**Scope.** Retired `C--Users-eric-rash*` slugs are excluded everywhere - the macOS\nslugs were re-keyed from them, so including them reports history as duplication.\n`handoffs/` and `transcripts/` are session records, not memory, and are never linted.\nThe LLM check defaults to `global-memory` + the `DEV` and `~` slugs, the ones that\nload together in a working session; `--all-slugs` widens it.\n\n**Similarity threshold.** Default 0.45. Measured 2026-09-01: the highest genuine\nwithin-slug pair scored 0.33. The report always prints the top pairs per slug so\nthe number can be retuned against evidence.\n\n**Report only.** memlint never edits a memory file. Merging and deleting memories\nstays a human decision.\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py                    # all checks\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py --structural-only  # skip LLM check (free)\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py --all-slugs        # widen the LLM check\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py --threshold 0.6    # retune near-duplicates\n```\n\nReports saved to `reports/memlint-YYYY-MM-DD.md`; `--structural-only` writes\n`memlint-YYYY-MM-DD-structural.md` instead, so a free run never clobbers the\nday's paid-for LLM findings. Runs automatically after the Sunday 02:30\nadjudication job.\n\n---\n\n## State Tracking\n\n`scripts/state.json` tracks:\n- `ingested` - map of daily log filenames to SHA-256 hashes, compilation timestamps, and costs\n- `query_count` - total queries run\n- `last_lint` - timestamp of most recent lint\n- `last_memlint` - timestamp of most recent memory-tier lint\n- `total_cost` - cumulative API cost\n\n`scripts/last-flush.json` tracks flush deduplication (session_id + timestamp).\n\nBoth are gitignored and regenerated automatically.\n\n---\n\n## Dependencies\n\n`pyproject.toml` (at project root):\n- `claude-agent-sdk>=0.1.29` - Claude Agent SDK for LLM calls with tool use\n- `python-dotenv>=1.0.0` - Environment variable management\n- `tzdata>=2024.1` - Timezone data\n- Python 3.12+, managed by [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/)\n\nNo API key needed - uses Claude Code's built-in credentials at `~/.claude/.credentials.json`.\n\n---\n\n## Costs\n\n| Operation | Cost |\n|-----------|------|\n| Compile one daily log | $0.45-0.65 |\n| Query (no file-back) | ~$0.15-0.25 |\n| Query (with file-back) | ~$0.25-0.40 |\n| Full lint (with contradictions) | ~$0.15-0.25 |\n| Structural lint only | $0.00 |\n| Full memlint (with contradictions) | ~$0.15-0.25 |\n| Structural memlint only | $0.00 |\n| Memory flush (per session) | ~$0.02-0.05 |\n\n---\n\n## Customization\n\n### Additional Article Types\n\nAdd directories like `people/`, `projects/`, `tools/` to `knowledge/`. Define the article format in this file (AGENTS.md) and update `utils.py`'s `list_wiki_articles()` to include them.\n\n### Obsidian Integration\n\nThe knowledge base is pure markdown with `[[wikilinks]]` - works natively in Obsidian. Point a vault at `knowledge/` for graph view, backlinks, and search.\n\n### Scaling Beyond Index-Guided Retrieval\n\nAt ~2,000+ articles / ~2M+ tokens, the index becomes too large for the context window. At that point, add hybrid RAG (keyword + semantic search) as a retrieval layer before the LLM. See Karpathy's recommendation of `qmd` by Tobi Lutke for search at scale.\n\n\n## Current Wiki Index\n\n# Knowledge Base Index\n\n| Article | Summary | Compiled From | Updated |\n|---------|---------|---------------|---------|\n| [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] | Adds 2026-07-09 GitHub MCP 401-on-reconnect: configured Authorization header disables OAuth fallback; fix is token refresh or header removal | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-14.md, 2026-06-03.md, 2026-06-09.md, 2026-07-09.md | 2026-08-01 |\n| [[concepts/custom-claude-code-skills]] | Three new skills (web-scraping, ingest-resource, improve-system) built for the SecondBrain vault; now cross-linked to an earlier Obsidian-vault-as-destination decision | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-21.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/daily-onramp-automation]] | Lean-mode on-ramp pipeline; now includes 2026-06-05 sparse-input run and cumulative ctx savings (4.0 MB / 22 days / 105 conversations, 214× compression before /compact) | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-14.md, 2026-06-05.md | 2026-06-16 |\n| [[concepts/catapult-connect-api-reference]] | Local Catapult Connect v6 API reference; now cross-linked to a mirror-image Perch API auth-scheme lesson from 2026-04-25 | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-14.md, 2026-04-25.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/keystone-os-tezino-project]] | KeystoneOS Tezino Phase 1 data-gap closure (CMJ/IMTP backfill, mRSI decline, Catapult token failure); now cross-linked to downstream Tezino_CampPrep SPC monitoring project initiated 2026-06-09 | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-14.md, 2026-04-25.md, 2026-06-09.md | 2026-06-16 |\n| [[connections/catapult-api-docs-and-tezino-token-issue]] | Same-day link between the Catapult API reference's token docs and the Tezino sync's stale-token failure | 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/web-scraping-skill-and-readme-doc-trick]] | Gap between the new web-scraping skill's routing tiers and the ReadMe .md/llms.txt technique that actually worked | 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] | Memory compiler setup; now includes version-skew failure mode (claude-agent-sdk bundled CLI diverges from system), fix via uv lock --upgrade-package, and Tier B independence confirmed | 2026-06-13.md | 2026-06-16 |\n| [[concepts/baylor-load-simulator-deployment]] | Baylor Load Simulator Vercel deployment; now includes the 2026-05-12 Comparison View feature and Catapult period-aggregation sync findings | 2026-04-14.md, 2026-04-24.md, 2026-05-12.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/skylar-zinnecker-cmj-rtc-reports]] | CMJ return-to-competition HTML reports for athlete Skylar Zinnecker, built from a VALD ForceDecks export, with redefined baseline and \"Post-Op Low\" terminology | 2026-04-14.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/catapult-token-friction-across-baylor-projects]] | Recurring Catapult OpenField token friction linking the Baylor Load Simulator deploy, the Tezino sync failure, and the Catapult API reference | 2026-04-14.md, 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/vald-forcedecks-cmj-data-across-projects]] | Shared VALD ForceDecks CMJ data and mRSI metric linking Skylar Zinnecker's RTC reports to the Tezino narrative backfill | 2026-04-14.md, 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]] | Round-7 batch import + data-quality repair; now records the \"verify prior background agents actually finished\" procedural lesson from 2026-04-22 | 2026-04-21.md, 2026-04-22.md, 2026-04-24.md | 2026-08-06 |\n| [[concepts/graphify-vault-separation]] | Decision to move graphify's 300+ generated files out of SportScienceResearchRepo and into the user's Obsidian vault | 2026-04-21.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/obsidian-vault-as-knowledge-hub]] | Obsidian SecondBrain as recurring knowledge destination: graphify cleanup, /ingest-resource skill, morning briefing durable copy, and Applied Performance OKR hub as canonical project home | 2026-04-21.md, 2026-06-10.md, 2026-05-21.md, 2026-06-01.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]] | \"The Monitor\" newsletter Issue 01 production: content verification, staff photo integration, preview-server rendering gotcha, and template-directory documentation | 2026-04-22.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] | Links a 2026-04-22 agent-citation-hallucination fix and a newsletter photo-rendering gotcha as instances of \"verify directly, don't trust the intermediate layer\" | 2026-04-22.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/perch-weight-room-domain-feature]] | Perch/Weight Room domain feature, now including its 2026-04-25 data-activation completion (436 athletes, 12,300 set records ingested via corrected Perch API integration) | 2026-04-24.md, 2026-04-25.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/subagent-driven-development]] | Two-stage review methodology (spec-compliance then code-quality) used to implement and verify Perch/Weight Room Task 9 | 2026-04-24.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/jinja2-none-safety-pattern]] | Jinja2's `default()` filter unreliably handles `None`; use explicit `is not none` guards instead | 2026-04-24.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/multi-stage-review-catches-agent-blind-spots]] | Multi-stage review principle extended to planning context: spec self-review catching carryover resolution behavioral gap | 2026-04-22.md, 2026-04-24.md, 2026-05-21.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/perch-api-integration-reference]] | Verified Perch API integration details (Bearer auth, /v3/sets pagination, 1RM formula, exercise/org IDs) that completed the Weight Room domain's data-activation step | 2026-04-25.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/api-credential-assumptions-can-be-backwards]] | Links a 2026-04-25 Perch auth-scheme surprise (Bearer worked, JWT didn't) to the 2026-06-10 Catapult token failure as mirror-image \"credential appearance is unreliable\" lessons | 2026-04-25.md, 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/baylor-load-simulator-comparison-view]] | Projected-vs-Actual ComparisonChart on /practices/[id]/compare, planned /comparison nav tab, single-metric scope; now cross-linked to the Color Triad Intended↔Realized leg | 2026-05-12.md | 2026-08-06 |\n| [[concepts/catapu